        "intuition": "Each step up the food chain, most of the energy is lost, so there is only room for a few big hunters at the top. The American ecologist Raymond Lindeman worked this out in 1942 by studying a small lake in Minnesota, counting how much of the energy captured by plants and algae reached each level of the food chain. A plant turns a small fraction of sunlight into sugar. A caterpillar eats the leaf, but most of what it eats is burned for movement and warmth, or passed out as waste, or never digested. Only a part becomes new caterpillar. The next eater gets a part of that. The rough rule of thumb is that about a tenth moves up each step, though real numbers vary a great deal between ecosystems. The result is a pyramid, wide at the bottom with grass, algae and plankton, narrow at the top with wolves, eagles and sharks. That is why there are far more rabbits than foxes, and why a whale that eats tiny krill can be so enormous: it feeds near the broad base. The same logic explains why raising grain for people feeds more of them than raising grain for cattle first. The pyramid is not a ranking of worth. It is an accounting of energy, and the books always balance.",
