        "intuition": "Your body is a thermostat for life, always nudging itself back toward the middle. The French physiologist Claude Bernard noticed in the nineteenth century that the fluid bathing our cells stays remarkably constant while the outside world swings wildly. He called it the milieu intérieur, and said its stability is the condition for a free life. In 1926 the American physiologist Walter Cannon gave the idea its name, homeostasis, meaning staying the same by changing. Your core temperature holds near 37 degrees Celsius. If you overheat, blood vessels near the skin widen and you sweat, and evaporation carries heat away. If you cool, vessels narrow and you shiver, and muscles burn fuel to warm you. After a meal your blood sugar rises, and the pancreas releases insulin, which tells cells to take the sugar in. Hours later, as it falls, the hormone glucagon releases stored reserves. In each case a sensor detects a drift, a control center compares it with a set point, and an effector pushes back. That is negative feedback: the correction opposes the change. The beauty is that nothing needs to understand the plan. The loop does the thinking. Health is not stillness. It is a constant, quiet, well-timed argument with the weather.",
