        "intuition": "Species do not just compete. They grow into each other, and some end up unable to live apart. Beneath a forest floor, fungi thread fine filaments through the soil and around tree roots. The fungus gathers water and minerals like phosphorus that the roots cannot reach easily, and the tree pays in sugars made by photosynthesis. This partnership, called mycorrhiza, is thought to be nearly as old as land plants themselves, hundreds of millions of years. Coral reefs run on a similar bargain: tiny algae live inside coral tissue, feeding the coral with sugars from sunlight, and in return receive shelter and nutrients. The German botanist Anton de Bary coined the word symbiosis in 1879, meaning living together. Some of the closest partnerships are inside you. The mitochondria in your cells, which burn fuel for energy, descend from free-living bacteria that were taken in by a larger cell more than a billion years ago. They still carry their own small ring of DNA. Cooperation can look soft, but it has been a powerful engine of evolution. Living things are not only individuals. They are neighborhoods, and a neighborhood can become a single body.",
