        "advanced": "Symbiosis covers a spectrum from mutualism, where both partners benefit, through commensalism to parasitism, and the lines move with conditions. Coevolution is the reciprocal evolutionary change that partners drive in each other; Paul Ehrlich and Peter Raven used butterflies and their host plants to frame the idea in 1964. The most consequential partnership is endosymbiosis. Lynn Margulis, in a 1967 paper published under the name Lynn Sagan, argued that mitochondria and chloroplasts were once free-living bacteria. The evidence is strong: both have their own circular DNA, divide on their own, and have membranes and ribosomes resembling bacterial ones, and genetic comparison places them among bacterial lineages. The origin of the complex cell, the eukaryote, is a merger. The limit is that cooperation is fragile; partners can cheat, and mutualisms can break, as in coral bleaching when heat expels the algae. Biologists now speak of the holobiont, a host and its microbes taken together. This is where nature and philosophy meet the question of what counts as one self."
