        "intuition": "Every species finds a way to make a living that others have left open, and that is how crowded places stay full of different lives. In 1934 the Russian biologist Georgy Gause grew two species of Paramecium, a single-celled pond animal, in the same dish with the same food. One drove the other to extinction. Grown separately, both thrived. The lesson, now called competitive exclusion, is that two species cannot indefinitely share exactly the same way of life in the same place. In the 1950s the American ecologist Robert MacArthur watched five species of warbler feed in the same spruce trees. They looked like direct rivals. But each foraged in a different part of the tree: some near the trunk, some at the branch tips, some high, some low. They had divided the tree. The ecologist G. Evelyn Hutchinson, in 1957, described the niche as a multi-dimensional space: temperature, food size, humidity, time of day, all the conditions under which a species can live. Competition pushes species to differ in at least one of those dimensions. Darwin's finches on the Galápagos show it in beaks: different sizes for different seeds. Nature looks like crowded generosity because everyone is, in some small way, doing something nobody else is doing quite as well.",
