        "intuition": "Some species hold up far more of an ecosystem than their numbers suggest, like the stone at the top of an arch. In 1963 the American ecologist Robert Paine began removing the purple sea star Pisaster from sections of rocky shore in Washington State. Within months mussels, which the sea stars normally ate, spread across the rock and crowded out almost everything else. The number of species in the cleared patches fell sharply. A single predator had been keeping the community diverse by holding back the strongest competitor. Paine named such animals keystone species in 1969. Sea otters offer another case: they eat sea urchins, which graze kelp. Without otters, urchins can strip kelp forests to bare rock; with them, the forests return, along with the fish and animals that live in them. These chains of influence, running down through the food web, are called trophic cascades. When wolves were returned to Yellowstone in 1995, elk behavior and numbers changed, and researchers have tracked effects on streamside plants, though how far the effects reach is still debated. The lesson holds anyway: in a living system, importance is not proportional to size. Quiet roles can carry the whole structure.",
