        "advanced": "A keystone species exerts an influence on community structure disproportionate to its abundance. Paine's 1966 paper, Food Web Complexity and Species Diversity, reported the Pisaster removal experiment on the Pacific coast, and the term keystone appeared in his 1969 paper. The mechanism there is predator-mediated coexistence: by consuming the dominant competitor, the predator frees space for others. The related idea of a trophic cascade came from Hairston, Smith and Slobodkin's 1960 argument about why the world is green, and was later documented by James Estes and colleagues in sea otter, urchin and kelp systems in the North Pacific. Not all keystones are predators: beavers and elephants engineer habitat, and some plants sustain whole animal guilds. The strongest counterpoint is that the concept is slippery. Effect size varies with context, and the Yellowstone case is still argued over, with elk, climate and hydrology all in the mix. The idea connects nature to political thought and to the old observation that a small thing can hold up a large one."
