        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "The Way We Were",
            "artist": "Barbra Streisand"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Emergence names properties of a system that arise from the interactions of its components and are not properties of any component alone. Weak emergence means the higher-level pattern is in principle derivable from the parts but only by simulating them, as in Reynolds's boids model (1986) or John Conway's Game of Life (1970), where three simple rules on a grid produce moving, self-copying shapes. Strong emergence, the claim that higher-level properties are in principle irreducible, is far more contested, and is sometimes invoked for consciousness. Anderson's 1972 essay argued that reductionism does not imply constructionism: knowing the laws of particles does not let you rebuild chemistry, biology or society from scratch. Stigmergy, indirect coordination through marks left in the environment, explains ant trails and termite mounds. The limit is that emergent is easily used as a label that explains nothing. It earns its keep only when the specific interactions are identified. This idea connects science to consciousness, and to the thought that mind itself may be a pattern of relationships."
