        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Lightning Crashes",
            "artist": "LIVE"
          },
          {
            "title": "Saturn",
            "artist": "Sleeping At Last"
          },
          {
            "title": "The Scientist",
            "artist": "Coldplay"
          },
          {
            "title": "Fast Car",
            "artist": "Tracy Chapman"
          },
          {
            "title": "Woodstock",
            "artist": "Joni Mitchell"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Homeostasis is the maintenance of a stable internal environment through regulated exchange with the outside, an idea traced to Claude Bernard's milieu intérieur and named by Walter Cannon in the 1920s. At the cellular level it relies on membrane transport: the sodium-potassium ATPase pumps three sodium ions out and two potassium ions in per cycle of ATP use, building the gradients that nerve and muscle cells depend on. At the organism level, buffers, respiration and kidney function hold blood pH within a narrow range, and hormones such as insulin, glucagon, antidiuretic hormone and aldosterone close the loops for sugar and water. Osmoregulation differs sharply across species, as with freshwater fish that absorb water and saltwater fish that lose it. The limit is that a fixed set point is a simplification: rhythms, fever, and stress responses shift targets deliberately. Failure of these loops underlies diabetes, kidney failure and heat stroke. This connects biology to physics through the second law, since keeping order costs energy."
