        "intuition": "Every living thing is made of cells, and every cell comes from another cell. In 1665 the English scientist Robert Hooke looked at a thin slice of cork under a microscope and saw rows of tiny boxes. He called them cells, after the small rooms of monks. They were dead walls, but the name stuck. Soon after, the Dutch merchant Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, grinding his own tiny lenses, saw living creatures in pond water and in a drop of his own mouth, and called them animalcules. In 1838 the botanist Matthias Schleiden concluded that plants are made of cells, and in 1839 his friend Theodor Schwann said the same of animals. In 1855 the physician Rudolf Virchow gave the third piece, often summed up as omnis cellula e cellula: every cell arises from a cell. That last idea matters most. It ended the old belief that life regularly springs from dead matter. Your body holds tens of trillions of cells, and each descends from the single fertilized egg you began as, and that from cells before it, in a chain that runs unbroken back through your ancestors to the first. You are a very old flame, passed hand to hand.",
        "links": [
          {
            "label": "Cell theory",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cell_theory"
          },
          {
            "label": "Cell",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cell_(biology)"
          }
        ],
        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Stay",
            "artist": "Zedd & Alessia Cara"
          },
          {
            "title": "Can't Help Falling in Love",
            "artist": "Haley Reinhart"
          },
          {
            "title": "10-20-30",
            "artist": "Big Sean"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Classical cell theory holds three claims: all organisms are composed of one or more cells; the cell is the basic unit of structure and function; and all cells arise from pre-existing cells. Hooke published his observations in Micrographia in 1665, Schleiden and Schwann formulated the first two claims in 1838 and 1839, and Virchow popularized the third in 1855, though Robert Remak had argued it earlier. Louis Pasteur's swan-neck flask experiments in 1861 gave decisive evidence against spontaneous generation. The theory has edge cases worth knowing. Viruses are not cells and do not reproduce independently. Some tissues are multinucleate, like skeletal muscle fibers, and red blood cells lose their nucleus. Moreover, the first cell must have arisen from non-living chemistry, so the third claim describes life as it is now, not its origin. The two broad kinds, prokaryotic and eukaryotic, differ by the presence of a nucleus and internal compartments. This connects biology to chemistry, and to the origin-of-life question that remains open."
