        "intuition": "Inside nearly every cell of your body is a molecule that stores instructions for building you, written in a four-letter alphabet. The letters are four chemical bases: adenine, thymine, guanine and cytosine. They form pairs, A with T and G with C, across the two strands of the double helix. In 1869 the Swiss chemist Friedrich Miescher first isolated a phosphorus-rich substance from the nuclei of white blood cells, and called it nuclein. For decades most scientists thought proteins must carry heredity. In 1944 Oswald Avery, Colin MacLeod and Maclyn McCarty showed that DNA transforms bacteria, and in 1952 Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase confirmed that DNA is the genetic material of a virus. In 1953 James Watson and Francis Crick described the double helix, relying on X-ray images made by Rosalind Franklin and measurements by Maurice Wilkins. The human genome holds about three billion base pairs in a single set. Stretched out, the DNA in one cell is around two meters long, packed into a nucleus far too small to see with the naked eye. The code is not magic and it is not a blueprint with every detail drawn. It is more like a recipe, and the cook is the cell.",
