        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Summer of '69",
            "artist": "Bryan Adams"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Ecological interdependence appears as trophic links, mutualisms, competition and shared use of nutrients. Tansley's 1935 definition of the ecosystem united organisms and environment in one system, and Eugene Odum's textbooks of the 1950s and 1960s spread the energy-flow view. Food webs are networks with structure: some species are highly connected, and the loss of such nodes can propagate through the network as secondary extinctions, a result explored in work by Dunne, Williams and Martinez and others. The robustness of a web depends on redundancy: many species perform overlapping roles, so removing one can be buffered. Pollination networks show nested structure, in which specialists tend to interact with generalists, which gives some resilience. The limit is that interdependence is not always benign; parasites, disease and competition are also connections. Humans are embedded: crops need pollinators and soil life, and the microbiome affects health. This links nature and biology to philosophical ideas of relation, and to the old Buddhist image of Indra's net."
