        "intuition": "The shape of DNA is the secret of how life copies itself. Two long strands wind around each other like a twisted ladder, and the rungs are pairs of chemical letters. Adenine always pairs with thymine, and guanine always pairs with cytosine. The rule had been hinted at by the biochemist Erwin Chargaff, who found around 1950 that in any DNA, the amount of adenine equals thymine and guanine equals cytosine, though no one yet knew why. The answer came in 1953. In London, Rosalind Franklin and her student Raymond Gosling had made an X-ray image of DNA fibers, known as Photo 51, with a clear X shape that told the trained eye the molecule was a helix. James Watson and Francis Crick, in Cambridge, saw it and built a model that fit: two strands running in opposite directions, with the letter pairs fitting in the middle like matching puzzle pieces. The helix is about two nanometers wide and makes a full turn roughly every ten rungs. The rungs are held by hydrogen bonds, strong enough in number to hold the strands together, but weak enough one by one that enzymes can unzip them. And because each strand predicts the other, a molecule can be copied by separating the strands and rebuilding each partner. The most important structure in biology is a lock whose key is its own mirror image.",
