        "intuition": "Life runs on a one-way street: DNA is copied into RNA, and RNA is read to build protein. The DNA stays safe in the nucleus like a master recipe book kept in a library. When the cell needs a protein, an enzyme called RNA polymerase copies the relevant gene into a disposable working copy, messenger RNA. The idea of a short-lived messenger was worked out in 1961 by Francois Jacob, Sydney Brenner and Matthew Meselson, and by Jacob and Jacques Monod. The messenger travels out to the ribosome, a molecular machine that reads it three letters at a time and assembles amino acids into a chain. How the reading worked was cracked soon after in a beautifully simple experiment. In 1961 Marshall Nirenberg and Heinrich Matthaei made an artificial RNA containing nothing but the letter uracil, UUUUU, and added it to a mixture of cell parts. The chain that came out was made only of the amino acid phenylalanine. The first word of the code had been read. The direction matters. The recipe can be copied into many working pages, and the working pages can be thrown away, but the cook's cooking cannot rewrite the recipe. Information flows from the archive to the action, and not the other way round.",
