        "intuition": "You inherited half of your genes from each parent, but you are not a blend of them. You are a new combination of their pieces. You have twenty-three pairs of chromosomes, one of each pair from your mother and one from your father. When your parents made the egg and the sperm that became you, each of them shuffled their two copies of every chromosome, swapping segments, and then dealt you one. Your sibling got a different deal, which is why siblings share on average about half their variants, not all. One sex chromosome pair decides much of the rest: two X chromosomes usually give a girl, and an X and a Y a boy. Mitochondria, the cell's power plants, carry a small ring of DNA of their own, and you inherited all of it from your mother. Some traits follow Gregor Mendel's simple rules, like certain blood disorders. But most do not. Height, for example, is strongly inherited, with twin studies finding that most of the variation between people comes from genes, yet it is shaped by thousands of small genetic differences, each nudging a little, plus nutrition and health. Eye color, once taught as a simple pair of dominant and recessive, is also influenced by many genes. You are not a copy and not a blend. You are a one-time arrangement of an ancestral deck.",
