        "intuition": "Your body is built by a molecular machine that reads a code and strings together beads. The beads are amino acids, of which life uses twenty. The machine is the ribosome, a complex of RNA and protein, which grips the messenger RNA and reads it three letters at a time. For each three-letter word, a small adapter molecule, called transfer RNA, carries in the matching amino acid, and the ribosome joins it to the growing chain. A bacterial ribosome adds around twenty amino acids a second. A surprising fact, shown in the structures solved by Venki Ramakrishnan, Thomas Steitz and Ada Yonath, who shared the 2009 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, is that the place where the chain is built is made of RNA, not protein. The ribosome is at heart an RNA enzyme, a clue that RNA may have preceded proteins in the early history of life. Then the chain folds. Christian Anfinsen showed in the early 1970s that a protein's sequence alone contains the instructions for its three-dimensional shape, and the problem of predicting that shape from the sequence took fifty years. In 2020 a program from DeepMind, AlphaFold, solved it for most proteins, and its creators shared the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry with David Baker. A line of letters becomes a living machine. That is how a recipe turns into a body.",
