        "intuition": "Bacteria, oak trees, mushrooms and people all speak the same genetic language, with only tiny dialect differences. The code is made of three-letter words called codons. With four letters, there are sixty-four possible words, and they spell out the twenty amino acids plus signals for stop. Most amino acids have more than one word, so the code has a built-in redundancy that cushions against some mistakes. In the early 1960s Marshall Nirenberg, working with Heinrich Matthaei, and then Har Gobind Khorana and Robert Holley, decoded it, and the three shared the 1968 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. The proof that it is universal is practical. In 1978 scientists at Genentech put a human gene for insulin into bacteria, and the bacteria read it correctly and made human insulin. If the code were different in bacteria, that could not have worked. That is also why a virus can hijack any cell: it speaks the same tongue. There are small exceptions. In human mitochondria, a word that normally means stop means tryptophan, which is evidence that those organelles were once separate bacteria with their own dialect. A language shared by every living thing is the strongest evidence that all living things share one beginning.",
