        "intuition": "Every time a cell divides, it copies three billion letters, and it makes almost no mistakes. The copying works by splitting the double helix and using each strand as a template. In 1958 Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl proved it with a clever experiment. They grew bacteria in heavy nitrogen, moved them to ordinary nitrogen, and used a centrifuge to sort the DNA by weight. After one round, all the DNA was a hybrid, half old and half new. That is called semiconservative copying: each daughter molecule keeps one old strand. The enzyme that builds the new strand is DNA polymerase, discovered by Arthur Kornberg in 1956. It reads along the template and adds the matching letter, checks it, and removes a wrong one if it has made a mistake. The result is startling. After proofreading and a second round of repair, the error rate is about one in a billion letters. Still, a quirk of chemistry means the enzyme can only build in one direction, so one strand is made in short pieces, called Okazaki fragments after the couple who found them in the 1960s, and stitched together. Also, each copy leaves the chromosome tips slightly shorter, and a protective cap called the telomere wears down, one of the clocks of aging. A perfect copy is not magic. It is a habit of carefulness, practiced a trillion times.",
