        "intuition": "On the largest scales, the universe is not a scatter of galaxies but a web: bright filaments, great empty voids, and dense knots where the threads meet. Galaxies cluster along sheets and strands, and between them lie vast regions with almost nothing. The first maps showed it. In the 1980s, surveys of galaxy positions by teams at the Harvard-Smithsonian Center for Astrophysics, led by Margaret Geller and John Huchra, found that galaxies are arranged on the surfaces of enormous bubbles, with empty space inside. In 1989 they described the Great Wall, a sheet of galaxies more than five hundred million light years long. Then computers were used to run the universe forward. Starting from tiny differences in density in the early universe, about one part in a hundred thousand as measured in the microwave background, simulations such as the Millennium Simulation of 2005 let gravity pull matter, mostly dark matter, into just such a web. Gas flows along the filaments into the knots, where galaxy clusters form. In 2014 Brent Tully and colleagues mapped our home supercluster, Laniakea, by the flows of galaxies. A 2020 study by Franco Vazza and Alberto Feletti noted striking similarities between the cosmic web and the network of neurons in a brain, though the resemblance is of pattern and not of function. Everything is connected, and the threads are made of gravity.",
