        "intuition": "The expansion of the universe is not slowing down. It is speeding up, and we do not know why. After the expansion was discovered, everyone assumed that gravity, pulling everything together, must be slowing it down. In the 1990s two teams set out to measure how much, by studying distant exploding stars, type Ia supernovae, whose brightness is known and so reveals how far away they are. In 1998 they found something that nobody had expected: the distant supernovae were fainter than expected, as if the expansion had been speeding up for the past several billion years. Saul Perlmutter, Brian Schmidt and Adam Riess shared the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics for the discovery. Something, which we call dark energy, is pushing space apart, and it makes up about two thirds of the universe's total energy. The simplest idea is a cosmological constant, a fixed energy of empty space itself. Einstein had added such a term to his equations in 1917 to hold the universe static, and then dropped it. It was an idea that turned out to deserve a second look. But quantum theory predicts a vacuum energy that is vastly larger than what we observe, and no one knows why the two are so far apart. The name is honest: we have measured the push and not yet found its source.",
