        "advanced": "Observations of Type Ia supernovae by the High-Z Supernova Search Team and the Supernova Cosmology Project showed in 1998 that cosmic expansion is accelerating, and this is supported independently by the microwave background, baryon acoustic oscillations and large-scale structure, giving a dark energy fraction of about 68 percent in the standard lambda cold dark matter model. The simplest explanation is Einstein's cosmological constant, introduced in 1917, with equation-of-state parameter w equal to minus one. Quantum field theory estimates of vacuum energy exceed the observed value by many orders of magnitude, perhaps as many as 120 in simple cutoff estimates, which is the cosmological constant problem. Recent baryon acoustic oscillation measurements from the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument, reported in 2024 and 2025, hint that dark energy may weaken over time, a result of moderate statistical significance that awaits confirmation. Dark energy connects cosmos to fundamental physics, and to the limits of what we know about empty space."
