        "intuition": "Our universe may be one of many, but this is an idea, not a finding. It arises from several different parts of physics. The first is cosmic inflation, proposed by Alan Guth in 1981: a burst of extremely rapid expansion in the first instant that explains why the universe is so smooth. In versions developed by Andrei Linde and Alexander Vilenkin, inflation, once started, may never stop everywhere, so new bubbles of space keep forming, each a separate universe with its own history. The second is string theory, which allows an enormous number of possible forms for the extra dimensions, each with different constants of nature, a set called the landscape. The third is a puzzle: some of the numbers in physics seem finely tuned for life. If there are many universes with varied constants, then we should not be surprised to find ourselves in one that suits us, since only such universes have observers. In 1987 Steven Weinberg used this reasoning to predict that the cosmological constant should be small but not zero, and the discovery of dark energy in 1998 fell in the range. But no one has ever observed another universe, and it may be that none can be observed. A theory that cannot be tested is not yet science. It is a question dressed as an answer, and we should keep wondering, with care.",
