        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Beautiful Things",
            "artist": "Benson Boone"
          },
          {
            "title": "Intuition",
            "artist": "Jewel"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Eudoxus's theory of proportion, preserved in Book V of Euclid's Elements, calls ratios a to b and c to d equal when, for all whole numbers m and n, m times a exceeds, equals or falls short of n times b exactly when m times c exceeds, equals or falls short of n times d. It never divides incommensurable lengths, and it foreshadows Richard Dedekind's 1872 construction of the real numbers by cuts. Scaling a figure by a factor k multiplies lengths by k, areas by k squared and volumes by k cubed, which is why proportion governs both art and engineering. The counterpoint is that proportion describes relationships and says nothing on its own about which ratios are beautiful; such claims need evidence beyond the arithmetic. Music is the oldest example: the octave is the ratio 2 to 1 and the fifth is 3 to 2. That ties this card to the art door, where measure becomes harmony."
