        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "If We Were Vampires",
            "artist": "Jason Isbell and the 400 Unit"
          },
          {
            "title": "Gut Feeling",
            "artist": "DEVO"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Iteration is the repeated application of a function f to its own output: x, f(x), f(f(x)), and so on. The central theorem is Stefan Banach's contraction mapping principle of 1922. If f shrinks all distances by a fixed factor below 1 on a complete space, then f has exactly one fixed point, and iterating from any start converges to it. The Babylonian square root method is Newton's method in disguise, and near its target it roughly squares the error each round. The counterpoint is that iteration offers no guarantee: it can diverge, settle into a cycle, or wander. The Collatz rule, halve if even and triple plus one if odd, has been checked for enormous starting values and still has no proof that every start reaches 1. Showing up daily works only when the daily step actually contracts toward something. This links mathematics to the nature door, where seasons and growth are iterations too."
