        "intuition": "Nature doesn't guess, it counts. And its favorite counting pattern is hidden in everything you love to look at. The rule is one line long: each number is the sum of the two before it, so 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34. Leonardo of Pisa, called Fibonacci, set it down in 1202 in a puzzle about breeding rabbits, though Indian scholars had met the same numbers earlier in studying poetic meter. The sequence hides small miracles. Add up the first five terms, 1 plus 1 plus 2 plus 3 plus 5, and you get 12, which is one less than the next-but-one term, 13. That holds forever. In a sunflower head the seeds sit in two families of spirals, one turning each way, and the counts are usually neighbors in the sequence, such as 34 and 55. The reason is packing. A plant that adds each new seed after a turn of about 137.5 degrees, the golden angle, never lines seeds up in rows, so the head fills evenly. The nautilus shell, often cited here, is a logarithmic spiral but not a golden one. The truth is better than the legend: the numbers appear where growth and packing demand them.",
