        "intuition": "The universe builds big complicated things using simple repeating rules. You are one of those rules. Take an equilateral triangle and remove the triangle formed by joining the midpoints of its sides. Three triangles remain. Do the same to each, forever. This is the Sierpinski triangle, described by Waclaw Sierpinski in 1915. After n rounds there are 3 to the n triangles, and the fraction of the original area that is left is three quarters raised to the n, which shrinks toward nothing. A shape with no area and yet an endless web of lines is a strange thing to hold in the mind. Its dimension is neither 1 nor 2. Since each piece is half the size and there are three of them, the dimension d satisfies 2 to the d equals 3, so d is log 3 over log 2, about 1.585. Fractal roughness is also measured in the real world. In the 1960s Mandelbrot built on Lewis Fry Richardson's observation that a coastline grows longer as your ruler gets shorter. A rule repeated at smaller scales makes the whole of the shape's richness.",
