        "advanced": "Mandelbrot defined a fractal in 1975 as a set whose Hausdorff dimension strictly exceeds its topological dimension. For an exactly self-similar set made of N copies scaled by a factor r, the similarity dimension is log N over log(1 over r). The Cantor set, removing middle thirds from a segment as Georg Cantor described in 1883, has dimension log 2 over log 3, about 0.631: more than a set of points, less than a line. Mandelbrot's 1967 paper asking how long the coast of Britain is, and his 1982 book The Fractal Geometry of Nature, showed that roughness at every scale is the rule for clouds, coastlines and lungs rather than the exception. The counterpoint is that natural objects are only statistically self-similar over a limited range of scales: a real fern stops at the cell. Mathematics describes the limit, and nature approximates it. This links to the nature door, where branching rivers, trees and bronchial tubes spend the same bargain of reach against volume."
