        "intuition": "When something looks perfect to you, there's a good chance 1.618 is hiding inside it. The honest version starts with a rectangle. Take one whose sides are in the golden ratio, for example 1.618 by 1. Cut a square off its end. The leftover rectangle measures 1 by 0.618, and 1 divided by 0.618 is 1.618 again. It is a golden rectangle too, smaller but the same shape. You can keep cutting forever, and the corners of the squares trace a spiral. That self-copying property is the heart of the number. It is also why 1.618 can never be written as a fraction: a fraction would give a smallest rectangle that cannot be cut again. The ratio also hides in the regular pentagon, where a diagonal divided by a side equals phi. In 1982 Dan Shechtman saw ten-fold diffraction patterns in a metal alloy, a symmetry once thought impossible in crystals, and quasicrystals were born, which share their geometry with Penrose tilings, where the ratio of two tile counts approaches phi. Phi is real and fascinating where it earns its place, and it is wise to doubt it where it is only claimed.",
