        "advanced": "The number phi satisfies phi squared equals phi plus 1, so every power of phi is built from the one before: phi to the n equals F(n) times phi plus F(n-1), with F the Fibonacci numbers. Euclid called it division in extreme and mean ratio, and Luca Pacioli's 1509 treatise On the Divine Proportion, illustrated by Leonardo da Vinci, made it famous in the Renaissance. Johannes Kepler wrote that geometry has two great treasures, the theorem of Pythagoras and the division of a line in extreme and mean ratio, and called the second a precious jewel. In the 1970s Roger Penrose found aperiodic tilings with two rhombi whose frequencies stand in the ratio phi. The counterpoint is that many alleged golden proportions in the Parthenon and in paintings come from selective measuring, and experiments on preference for golden rectangles are mixed at best. The mathematics is certain; the folklore needs checking. This links to the art door."
