        "advanced": "A sequence with constant difference d has terms a plus n d and partial sum n times (first plus last) over 2. If the absolute value of r is below 1 the infinite sum converges to a over (1 minus r), so one half plus a quarter plus an eighth, continuing, equals 1, which resolves Zeno's paradox. Jacob Bernoulli found the number e in 1683 by asking what happens to 1 times (1 plus 1 over n) to the n as compounding becomes continuous. John Napier's logarithms of 1614 turn a geometric progression into an arithmetic one, which is why they sped up calculation and why decibels and the Richter scale count in steps. The counterpoint is that real exponential growth always meets a limit. Pierre Verhulst's logistic equation of 1838 adds the ceiling, and growth bends into an S. This links to the nature door, where populations, cells and epidemics all begin geometric and end bounded."
