        "intuition": "When things go away, they often find a way to come back around. That's not coincidence, that's a cycle. The simplest cycle is a swing, and the mathematics of it is a single sentence: the farther you are pushed from rest, the harder you are pulled back, in proportion. That rule, force equals minus k times displacement, produces simple harmonic motion, the smooth wave called a sine. Galileo noticed, watching a swinging lamp, that a pendulum takes about the same time for a wide arc as for a small one, and Christiaan Huygens used this in 1656 to build the first accurate pendulum clock. For small swings the period is 2 pi times the square root of length over gravity. A pendulum one meter long swings through one full cycle in about 2 seconds. A guitar string works the same way, vibrating at a base frequency and at whole-number multiples of it, the harmonics. In 1822 Joseph Fourier showed that any repeating pattern, however jagged, can be built by adding sine waves of those frequencies. Under the many rhythms of the world there is one rhythm, stacked.",
