        "advanced": "Simple harmonic motion solves x'' = minus omega squared times x, with solutions A cos(omega t plus a phase), period 2 pi over omega. Fourier's 1822 Analytic Theory of Heat showed that a periodic function can be written as a sum of sines and cosines, the basis of signal processing, audio compression and quantum wave mechanics. The Fourier transform extends this to non-repeating signals. The counterpoint is that many things called cycles are not periodic. Business and history cycles are loose recurrences with changing lengths, and a pattern seen in a short record may be chance. Nonlinear systems can produce limit cycles, like the beating heart, that return to the same rhythm even after being disturbed, which is a sturdier kind of periodicity than the pendulum. Where a cycle is exact, as in an atomic clock locked to the vibration of cesium atoms, it becomes the standard of time itself. This links to the nature door, where day, season and tide set the beat."
