        "advanced": "A symmetry of an object is a transformation preserving its structure, and the symmetries of any object form a group. Felix Klein's 1872 Erlangen Program defined geometry itself as the study of properties unchanged under a group of transformations. Euclid's Elements ends by proving there are exactly five regular convex solids, a classification that rests on symmetry: the angles meeting at a vertex must total less than a full turn. The 17 wallpaper groups were proved complete by Fedorov in 1891 and independently by George Polya in 1924, and the border patterns that repeat in one direction number exactly seven. The counterpoint is that perfect symmetry is often sterile: structure appears when symmetry breaks, as when a uniform cooling liquid freezes into a crystal with a direction, and the universe's variety depends on broken symmetries. A face that is perfectly symmetrical looks less alive than a real one. This links to the science door, where Noether's theorem turns every symmetry into a conservation law."
