        "intuition": "Imagine walking straight forward on a giant ball; eventually, without ever turning, you would end up right back where you started. On a sphere the straight lines are great circles, the paths a flight takes between continents, and this changes the rules. Start at the North Pole, walk down to the equator, turn a right angle, walk a quarter of the way around, turn a right angle again, and walk back up to the pole. You have drawn a triangle with three right angles, so its angles add to 270 degrees. In flat geometry that is impossible. Albert Girard found in 1629 that the extra is tied to area: the excess in degrees is the area divided by the radius squared, converted to degrees. On a sphere there are also no parallel lines, because any two great circles cross. Around 1830 Nikolai Lobachevsky and Janos Bolyai independently described the opposite world, where through a point there are many parallels and triangle angles sum to less than 180 degrees, and Gauss had privately explored it earlier. The flat plane is only one possible space, and the universe gets to decide which one it is.",
