        "advanced": "Two-dimensional geometries with constant curvature come in three types: positive curvature, like the sphere, where triangle angles sum to more than 180 degrees; zero, the Euclidean plane, where they sum to exactly 180; and negative, the hyperbolic plane, where they sum to less. Bernhard Riemann's 1854 lecture extended the idea to curved spaces of any dimension, in which curvature can vary from point to point, and Eugenio Beltrami in 1868 and Henri Poincare later built models that showed hyperbolic geometry is as consistent as Euclid's. Einstein's 1915 theory of general relativity uses Riemann's mathematics, with mass and energy curving spacetime. The counterpoint is that curvature is something a creature on the surface can measure without looking from outside, by checking its triangles, which is why Gauss called the result his remarkable theorem. Observations of our own cosmos so far are consistent with space being very nearly flat on the largest scales. This links to the science door, where geometry is part of gravity."
