        "intuition": "Imagine a string tied to your belly button and a pole; as you walk around the pole, you feel the constant, never-ending curve of the perfect path. That path is a circle, and its one great constant is pi: the circumference divided by the diameter, the same for every circle from a coin to an orbit. Archimedes around 250 BC trapped it between 223 over 71 and 22 over 7 by fitting polygons with 96 sides inside and outside a circle, which gives pi between about 3.1408 and 3.1429. Pi is not a fraction, as Johann Lambert showed in 1761, and in 1882 Ferdinand von Lindemann proved it is not the root of any polynomial with whole-number coefficients, which ended the old dream of squaring the circle with compass and straightedge. Yet simple sums reach it. Gregory and Leibniz found that 1 minus a third plus a fifth minus a seventh, continued forever, equals pi over 4, and Indian mathematician Madhava found it centuries earlier. Circular motion uses it every moment: walk a circle of radius r once and you cover 2 pi r. An endless number sits inside every closed loop.",
