        "intuition": "Imagine your skin is a single continuous sheet that covers your whole body; no matter how you move, the connectivity of your skin stays the same. Topology studies exactly that: what survives stretching, bending and twisting, without cutting or gluing. A coffee cup and a donut are the same to a topologist, because each has exactly one hole and one can be molded into the other. It began in 1736 when Leonhard Euler considered the seven bridges of Konigsberg and asked whether one could cross each exactly once. He saw that only the connections mattered, not distances, and that a walk crossing every bridge once can have at most two landmasses with an odd number of bridges. All four had odd counts, three with 3 bridges and one with 5, so no such walk exists. Later he noticed that for any convex solid, vertices minus edges plus faces equals 2: a cube has 8 minus 12 plus 6. That number does not change however you reshape the solid, so it measures something deep. A similar fact says you cannot comb the hair flat on a sphere without leaving a cowlick. Topology is the mathematics of what cannot be removed by any amount of gentle reshaping.",
