        "intuition": "Imagine you are standing in a hall of mirrors, and you see a small version of yourself inside a small version of yourself, going on forever into the distance. A fractal can be built to do exactly that, and the Menger sponge shows what happens to space. Start with a cube. Cut it into 27 small cubes, three along each edge, and remove the one in the center and the six at the middle of each face. 20 cubes are left. Do the same to each of those, and keep going. After n rounds there are 20 to the n cubes, each with side one third to the n. The volume left is 20 over 27 raised to the n, which shrinks toward nothing, while the surface area grows without limit. The sponge is all holes and still a connected solid. There is also a way to build a fractal by chance. Mark three corners of a triangle, pick a point, and repeatedly jump halfway toward a randomly chosen corner. The dots, though random, trace out the Sierpinski triangle. The pattern is not in any single jump, but in the rule they all obey.",
