        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Satisfied",
            "artist": "Hamilton Cast"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Many fractals are attractors of iterated function systems: a few contracting maps applied again and again. John Hutchinson proved in 1981, using Banach's contraction principle, that such a system has a unique non-empty compact set that is exactly the union of its own shrunken copies. That explains the chaos game, since every random jump is applying one of the maps, and Michael Barnsley's 1988 book Fractals Everywhere popularized it, with the famous fern. The Menger sponge, described by Karl Menger in 1926, has dimension log 20 over log 3, about 2.727, between surface and solid. The counterpoint is about use: such constructions are mathematically exact but physical objects only follow them over a few scales, and a fractal dimension is a summary, not an explanation of why a pattern formed. Where the idea is truly powerful is compression and modeling, because a small rule can regenerate an immense structure. This links to the art door, where recursive form, from Gothic tracery to digital landscapes, follows the same economy."
