        "intuition": "Imagine you are a 2D shadow on the wall; suddenly, someone lifts you off the wall into the room; everything you knew just got a whole new side. In 1884 Edwin Abbott wrote a story, Flatland, about exactly this, and mathematics lets you follow it upward. Dimension counts how many numbers you need to name a point: one for a line, two for a floor, three for a room. A square has 4 corners, a cube has 8, and in general a cube in n dimensions has 2 to the n corners. Build the step up by dragging. Drag a point and you get a line with 2 ends. Drag the line and you get a square with 4 corners and 4 edges. Drag the square and you get a cube with 8 corners, 12 edges and 6 faces. Drag the cube through a fourth direction and you get a tesseract, with 16 corners, 32 edges, 24 square faces and 8 cubes as its boundary. We cannot picture it, but we can count it exactly. The cube you hold casts a flat shadow, and the shadow shows only some of what the cube is. Each higher world holds more than the lower one can show.",
