        "advanced": "A space has dimension n when n independent coordinates specify each point; in linear algebra, the dimension of a vector space is the number of vectors in any basis. In 1877 Georg Cantor found a one-to-one matching between the points of a line and the points of a square, and wrote to Richard Dedekind that he could see it but could not believe it. If dimension were mere counting of points, it would mean nothing, and in 1911 L. E. J. Brouwer proved that continuous matchings preserve it, rescuing the idea. Bernhard Riemann's 1854 lecture allowed any number of dimensions, and in 1919 Theodor Kaluza proposed a fifth to unify gravity and electromagnetism. String theory requires 10 or 11, but no experiment has shown any of the extra dimensions. The counterpoint is that mathematics permits any dimension while nature may offer exactly three of space. This links to the consciousness door, where the question of which view reveals more has always been asked."
