        "intuition": "You feel the presence of the answer before you calculate it, like a humming in your chest that gets louder as you get closer to the truth. A letter standing in for a number is how algebra holds that feeling still long enough to work with it. The idea is younger than counting. Diophantus of Alexandria, around 250 AD, used a special sign for the unknown, and in about 820 Muhammad al-Khwarizmi wrote the book whose title gave algebra its name, al-jabr, meaning restoration. But the letters we know came later. In 1591 Francois Viete began using vowels for unknown quantities and consonants for known ones, and in 1637 Rene Descartes switched to the habit we still keep: x, y and z for what we seek, a, b and c for what we are given. A variable does two jobs. Sometimes it is an unknown waiting to be found, as in 3x plus 5 equals 20. Other times it is a quantity that really changes. If you are paid 12 dollars an hour, then pay P equals 12 times hours h, and h can be any number you like. One letter now describes every possible week at once.",
