        "intuition": "A feeling of rightness or settling in your gut when a problem is finally solved and the balance is restored. The equals sign has a surprisingly human origin. In 1557 the Welsh mathematician Robert Recorde, in a book called The Whetstone of Witte, drew two short parallel lines and explained that no two things could be more equal. Before that, writers spelled out the words. What the sign promises is a balance: whatever you do to one side you must do to the other. Take 3x plus 5 equals 20. Subtract 5 from both sides and you have 3x equals 15. Divide both sides by 3 and x equals 5. Put 5 back into the original: 3 times 5 plus 5 is 20, so both pans of the scale hold the same weight. Equality also obeys three plain laws. Everything equals itself. If a equals b then b equals a. And if a equals b and b equals c then a equals c. They sound too obvious to state, yet every solved equation rests on them. Fairness in mathematics is not a feeling. It is a rule applied the same way on both sides, every time.",
