        "advanced": "Equality satisfies reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity, which together make it an equivalence relation, and it also obeys substitution: if a equals b, then anything true of a is true of b. In set theory the axiom of extensionality defines equality of sets: two sets are equal exactly when they have the same members. A subtle point is that equal does not mean written the same way: 0.999 repeating and 1 are two names for one real number, since the difference is smaller than every positive number. The counterpoint appeared around 2010, when Vladimir Voevodsky's univalence axiom pushed mathematicians to treat equality as a structure that can have many different proofs, not a simple yes or no, a view at the center of homotopy type theory. In ordinary algebra the lesson is humbler. Each step must be reversible or checked, since squaring both sides can create solutions the original lacked. This links to the philosophy door, where identity is an ancient puzzle."
