        "advanced": "A function has an inverse exactly when it is a bijection: each output comes from exactly one input. Inverses reverse composition, so the inverse of doing f then g is doing the inverse of g and then the inverse of f. John Napier's logarithms of 1614 gave an inverse to exponentiation and made multiplication into addition. The fundamental theorem of calculus, built by Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz in the late 1600s, states that differentiation and integration are inverse operations up to a constant. The counterpoint belongs to computing and physics: some processes are easy to do and hard to undo. Multiplying two large primes is quick, while factoring the product is believed to be hard, and this gap is the basis of public-key cryptography. In nature, stirring cream into coffee is easy to do and effectively impossible to reverse, which is the second law of thermodynamics at human scale. This links to the science door, where the arrow of time is an inverse we cannot find."
