        "intuition": "The feeling of a lightbulb going off when a bunch of random thoughts finally click into a realization. In algebra that click has two stages. An expression is a phrase, such as 3x plus 2. It has no verdict, because it does not claim anything. An equation joins two expressions with an equals sign and so makes a claim, which can be true or false depending on x. The claim 3x plus 2 equals 11 is true only when x is 3. The claim x plus 1 equals x plus 2 is true for no x at all. And the claim (x plus 1) squared equals x squared plus 2x plus 1 is true for every x, which is called an identity. So an equation can have one answer, none, or all. The number of answers also follows a law. Carl Friedrich Gauss proved in 1799 what is called the fundamental theorem of algebra: an equation of degree n, the highest power of x being n, has exactly n solutions if you allow complex numbers and count repeats. A line equation has one, a quadratic has two, a cubic has three. The structure of the question determines how many truths it can hold.",
