        "advanced": "Formally, an expression is a term built from numbers, variables and operations, and an equation is a statement that two terms are equal. Babylonian scribes solved quadratics by 1800 BC, Brahmagupta gave a general solution in 628, and the cubic formula was published by Gerolamo Cardano in 1545 after Tartaglia disclosed it to him. The work of Niels Henrik Abel in 1824 and Evariste Galois in the early 1830s showed that no formula using roots solves the general equation of degree five or more. The counterpoint to the hope that every equation can be solved is stronger still: in 1970 Yuri Matiyasevich completed the work showing that no algorithm can decide whether an arbitrary polynomial equation in whole numbers, a Diophantine equation, has a solution, settling Hilbert's tenth problem. Some questions that can be asked clearly can never be answered by any method. This links to the philosophy door, where the line between a statement and a claim is the subject of logic."
