        "intuition": "We are all made of the same atoms, just combined in unique, miraculous ways. Combinatorics is the exact counting of such combinations, and its numbers become enormous quickly. How many ways can n different things be put in a row? The first place can be filled n ways, the next n minus 1, and so on, giving n factorial. A deck of 52 cards can be arranged in 52 factorial ways, which is about 8 times 10 to the 67th. That is so large that, in practice, a well-shuffled deck has almost certainly never been in that order before in all of history. Choosing without caring about order is different. The number of ways to choose k things from n is n factorial over k factorial times (n minus k) factorial. To choose 6 numbers from 49 in a lottery gives 13,983,816 possible tickets, so any one ticket has a chance of 1 in nearly 14 million. These numbers sit in Pascal's triangle, where each entry is the sum of the two above it, as Blaise Pascal described in 1654, though the triangle was known earlier in India, Persia and China. Row n of the triangle sums to 2 to the n. A few simple building blocks and a few simple rules give a wealth of different results.",
        "links": [
          {
            "label": "Introduction to Combinatorics",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combinatorics"
          },
          {
            "label": "Permutation",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permutation"
          }
        ],
        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Sittin' on the Dock of the Bay",
            "artist": "Otis Redding"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Counting principles rest on the rule of product and the rule of sum, and from them come permutations, combinations and the binomial theorem: (x plus y) to the n expands with coefficients from row n of Pascal's triangle. Inclusion-exclusion counts a union by correcting for overlaps. The pigeonhole principle, stated by Peter Dirichlet in 1834, says that if more than n objects go into n boxes, some box holds two. It is the seed of Ramsey theory, which proves that complete disorder is impossible: among any six people, there are three who all know each other or three who are strangers to one another. The counterpoint is combinatorial explosion: counts grow faster than any computer, which is why the problem of finding the best route among many cities stays hard. This links to the nature door, where the four letters of DNA produce a number of sequences of length n equal to 4 to the n."
