        "intuition": "Even when something looks empty, it still has a very important job. The nothing between the somethings gives them meaning. Consider the numbers 35, 305 and 3,005. Without a symbol for an empty place, all three would be written 35, and a reader could only guess. Roman numerals dodged the problem by giving every size its own letter, which is why arithmetic in Roman numerals is so clumsy: you cannot line up columns that do not exist. Place value is the better idea. In 305 the digits mean 3 hundreds, 0 tens and 5 ones, and the zero is not a quantity, it is a hand holding the tens place open so the 3 can stay in the hundreds. Babylonian scribes began marking an empty place with a pair of wedges around 300 BC, and the Maya independently used a shell sign for zero in their calendar. In India the idea matured into a full number system. The oldest dated Indian zero symbol is a dot in the Bakhshali manuscript, which radiocarbon testing has placed centuries before the year 800, though that dating is disputed. Place value turns a few digits into any number you like, but only because one of those digits is allowed to mean nothing.",
