        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "I'll Be Missing You (feat. Faith Evans & 112)",
            "artist": "Puff Daddy"
          },
          {
            "title": "Work Hard, Play Hard",
            "artist": "Wiz Khalifa"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Boolean algebra on the values 0 and 1, George Boole's 1854 invention, is the algebra of switching circuits, as Claude Shannon showed in 1937. Binary addition is XOR for each digit plus a carry, and arithmetic modulo 2 forms the smallest field, which Richard Hamming used in 1950 to build error-correcting codes: extra check bits let a machine detect and repair a flipped bit. Rolf Landauer argued in 1961 that erasing one bit must release at least k times T times ln 2 of heat, about 3 times 10 to the minus 21 joules at room temperature, a bound that experiments have since tested. The counterpoint is that a physical zero is never exactly zero: a voltage is low enough, and noise margins decide where 0 ends and 1 begins, so digital reliability is engineered out of analog fuzziness. The apparent perfection of the digital is a human achievement, not a given. This links to the science door, where thermodynamics and information turned out to be the same subject."
