        "intuition": "Even at ultimate zero, quantum mechanics says particles never truly stop. Zero-point energy remains. True nothing is impossible. The lowest temperature has an exact number. Experiments in the 1700s and 1800s found that a gas shrinks by about 1 part in 273 of its volume at 0 degrees Celsius for each degree of cooling, so extended backward its volume would vanish at about negative 273 degrees. In 1848 William Thomson, later Lord Kelvin, proposed measuring temperature from that point, and today it is exactly negative 273.15 Celsius, written 0 kelvin. On this scale temperature is proportional to the average energy of motion: for a simple gas the average kinetic energy per particle is three halves of k times T, so halving the kelvin temperature halves the energy. Yet you can never reach the bottom. The third law of thermodynamics, formulated by Walther Nernst around 1906, implies that no finite series of steps can take a system exactly to 0 kelvin. Physicists have cooled atoms to a few billionths of a degree above it, and in 1995 formed a new state of matter, the Bose-Einstein condensate, in which thousands of atoms act as a single wave. Liquid helium never freezes at ordinary pressure, however cold, because its zero-point motion is too lively.",
