        "intuition": "Your gain plus their loss always equals zero. The universe keeps score, and the total is always balanced. That is true only in a special kind of game, and mathematics can say exactly which. A zero-sum game is one where whatever one player wins, the other loses, so the payoffs always add to zero. In matching pennies, you win a dollar if the coins match and lose one if they differ. If you always choose heads, your opponent learns it and always chooses tails. The best you can do is mix, playing heads half the time at random. John von Neumann proved in 1928 that in every two-player zero-sum game with finitely many choices, there is a best mixture for each player, and the two bests agree on a value, called the value of the game. For matching pennies the value is 0. For rock, paper, scissors, play each one third of the time. Most of life is not like this. If I trade my extra apples for your extra oranges, we both end up better off, and the total grew. The prisoner's dilemma, formulated in 1950 by Merrill Flood and Melvin Dresher, shows what happens when we treat a non-zero-sum game as a zero-sum one. Seeing which kind of game you are in changes how you play.",
        "links": [
          {
            "label": "Game Theory Academy",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_theory"
          },
          {
            "label": "Zero-sum game",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero-sum_game"
          }
        ],
        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Hoppípolla",
            "artist": "Sigur Rós"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Von Neumann's minimax theorem of 1928 says that for a finite two-player zero-sum game, the maximum over mixed strategies of the minimum expected payoff equals the minimum over the other player's mixed strategies of the maximum, so there is a well-defined value. His 1944 book with Oskar Morgenstern, Theory of Games and Economic Behavior, founded game theory. John Nash's 1950 theorem extended the existence of equilibrium to finite games of any kind, and in the zero-sum case it coincides with minimax. The counterpoint is that zero-sum thinking is a well-known bias: in 1980 Robert Axelrod's computer tournaments for repeated prisoner's dilemma found that a simple cooperative rule, tit for tat, outscored aggressive ones, since in repeated non-zero-sum games cooperation can pay. The sum being zero is a property of the payoffs, not of the world. This links to the nature door, where John Maynard Smith's evolutionary game theory describes how animals settle on strategies."
