        "intuition": "You carry the patterns of the whole world inside you. The part contains the whole. As above, so below. In mathematics that old phrase has a precise form. A shape is self-similar when it is made of smaller copies of itself. Take the Koch curve: each piece is one third the size of the whole and there are four of them. A Romanesco broccoli, the green spiraling one, shows the idea in a kitchen, where a floret looks like a smaller head, though only for a few rounds, until the cells begin. Pure mathematical objects can go on forever. Some natural things are self-similar only in a statistical sense. In the 1950s the hydrologist Harold Hurst studied the floods of the Nile and found that the pattern of high and low years looked alike at different time scales. Brownian motion, the jitter of a pollen grain, has a clean version of this: look at a path over four times as long a period, and you must stretch the distance by only a factor of 2, the square root of 4, and the path looks like the same kind of random walk. No exact copies appear, but the character of the roughness stays the same as you zoom in or out. That is a deeper kind of resemblance than matching shapes.",
