        "advanced": "A set is strictly self-similar when it is a union of N copies of itself, each scaled by a ratio r below 1, and its similarity dimension is log N over log of 1 over r. Hurst's 1951 study of reservoir design on the Nile gave the exponent that bears his name, a measure of how persistent a series is, and Benoit Mandelbrot built much of his later work on it. Power laws are the signature of such scaling: they have no preferred size. The counterpoint is about overreach. The phrase as above, so below comes from the Hermetic Emerald Tablet, and it is a statement about correspondence in meaning, which mathematics does not support in general: the laws governing a galaxy and an atom differ, and self-similarity holds only where there is no natural length to break it. Where it does hold, as in river networks and turbulence, it is a clue to the underlying process. This links to the mysticism door, where the old claim gets its home."
