        "intuition": "Big beautiful things come from doing one small simple thing over and over with love. The recipe calls for itself. A recursive process solves a problem by solving a smaller copy of the same problem. The classic is the Tower of Hanoi, a puzzle invented by Edouard Lucas in 1883, with three pegs and a stack of disks of different sizes, to be moved one at a time without ever placing a bigger disk on a smaller one. To move n disks, first move the top n minus 1 out of the way, then move the biggest, then move the n minus 1 back on top. So the number of moves T for n disks satisfies T(n) equals 2 times T(n minus 1) plus 1, with T of 1 equal to 1, and the answer is 2 to the n minus 1. Three disks need 7 moves. Ten need 1,023. A legend says monks move 64 gold disks, and that the world ends when they finish. At one move per second, 2 to the 64th minus 1 moves take more than 500 billion years. Every recursion needs a stopping point, the base case, such as the single disk that can just be moved. Without it the story never ends. With it, a rule too simple to be impressive does a task far too large to finish by hand.",
