        "advanced": "A fixed point x star of f satisfies f of x star equals x star, and it attracts nearby iterations when the absolute value of the derivative there is below 1. Feigenbaum's constant, about 4.6692, is universal: it appears in a whole class of one-humped maps and in real experiments. Tien-Yien Li and James Yorke proved in 1975 that period three implies chaos, and Oleksandr Sharkovskii had proved a stronger ordering of periods in 1964, long unread in the West. Robert May's 1976 Nature article brought the logistic map to ecologists as a warning that simple models can behave wildly. The counterpoint is that in a chaotic regime the rule is deterministic but long-term prediction is impossible, since small errors grow exponentially, and a simple rule does not mean a simple outcome. Daily habits compound differently: not all repetition converges, and the choice of function matters. This links to the nature door, where populations rise and fall by exactly such feedback."
