        "intuition": "In a tiny dot, a whole universe of beauty waits. Simple rules create infinite complexity. The rule is this. Pick a number c. Start at 0, then repeatedly square the current number and add c. The Mandelbrot set is the collection of all c for which the numbers never run away to infinity. Try c equal to 0: you stay at 0 forever, so it is in. Try c equal to 1: you get 0, 1, 2, 5, 26, and it explodes, so 1 is out. Try c equal to negative 1: you get 0, negative 1, 0, negative 1, a steady swing, so it is in. A neat fact makes this checkable: once any value passes 2 in size, it escapes for good. Here c can be a complex number, with a real and an imaginary part, so the set lives on a flat plane, and coloring points by how fast they escape gives the famous images. Benoit Mandelbrot first saw it on the computers at IBM in 1980. Adrien Douady and John Hubbard proved in the early 1980s that the set is a single connected piece, and in 1998 Mitsuhiro Shishikura proved that its boundary is so crinkled that it has dimension 2. From a rule short enough to fit on a napkin, an endless landscape of detail unfolds.",
