        "advanced": "The Mandelbrot set M is the set of complex c for which the orbit of 0 under z goes to z squared plus c stays bounded, and orbits that ever exceed absolute value 2 escape. Douady and Hubbard proved that M is connected in 1982 to 1985 by constructing a conformal map from the outside of M to the outside of a disk. Shishikura's 1998 theorem says the boundary of M has Hausdorff dimension 2. The central open question is whether M is locally connected, the MLC conjecture, which would imply that its fine structure is fully describable. The counterpoint to the thumbprint of God idea is that the pictures are computed with finite iterations and finite precision, so what we see is an approximation of a perfect mathematical set. The beauty is real, though, and it is a theorem: the complexity was not added, it was forced by the rule. This links to the art door, where the images became part of how people see mathematics."
