        "intuition": "The laws governing the small mirror the large. As above, so below. Size is just a frame, the pattern is eternal. That is half the truth, and the other half is just as interesting. A function shows scaling invariance when changing the scale only multiplies it by a constant factor: f of 2x equals 2 to the k times f of x. These are the power laws, and they have no preferred size, which is why a photograph of a rock can be mistaken for a mountain. Earthquakes follow such a pattern. By the Gutenberg-Richter law, each step up of one magnitude unit brings roughly ten times fewer earthquakes, and each unit of magnitude means about 32 times more energy released. But size does matter, and Galileo explained why in 1638 in his Two New Sciences. Double an animal's length and its weight grows 8-fold because volume goes as length cubed, while the strength of its bones grows only 4-fold because it goes as cross-section area. An ant scaled to the size of an elephant would collapse. In 1932 Max Kleiber found a curious pattern in the metabolic rates of animals, which scale as body mass to the power three quarters. Scale invariance is a pattern you find in some things. Scale breaking is a pattern you find in the rest.",
