        "advanced": "A function is homogeneous of degree k if f of lambda x equals lambda to the k times f of x, and power laws are the scale-free solutions. The renormalization group, developed by Kenneth Wilson in the early 1970s and honored with the 1982 Nobel Prize in Physics, explains why systems near a critical point, such as water at its critical temperature, look the same at every magnification and share universal exponents regardless of microscopic details. The counterpoint is warranted skepticism. Many data sets claimed as power laws are not: in 2009 Aaron Clauset, Cosma Shalizi and Mark Newman showed that statistical tests often cannot distinguish a true power law from other heavy-tailed shapes, and that a straight line on a log-log plot is weak evidence. Kleiber's three-quarter law has been explained by network theory, though the explanation is still debated. This links to the science door, where universality classes are among the most surprising results of the twentieth century."
