        "intuition": "You are a little universe, carrying the light of everything inside you. Separation is an illusion of scale. A real hologram does have the property in the simple version: cut the plate and each piece still shows the whole image, only blurrier, because every point of the plate records light from the whole scene. Physics found something stranger. In 1972 Jacob Bekenstein argued that a black hole's entropy, its count of hidden possibilities, grows with the area of its horizon and not its volume. In 1974 Stephen Hawking showed that black holes radiate, which fixed the formula: the entropy equals the horizon area divided by four, measured in units of the Planck area, a square about 1.6 times 10 to the minus 35 meters on a side. For a black hole with the mass of the Sun, that comes to roughly 10 to the 77th in natural units, far above the entropy of the Sun itself. If the most information a region can hold is set by its surface, then perhaps all that happens inside can be described on the boundary. Gerard 't Hooft proposed this in 1993 and Leonard Susskind named it the holographic principle. The universe may store its depth in its surface.",
