        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Agape",
            "artist": "Bear's Den"
          },
          {
            "title": "All for You (Full Band Version)",
            "artist": "Sister Hazel"
          },
          {
            "title": "Aquarius",
            "artist": "The 5th Dimension"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "The crystallographic restriction theorem says a lattice can have rotation symmetry only of order 1, 2, 3, 4 or 6, since the trace of a rotation matrix in a lattice basis is an integer, 2 cos of 2 pi over n, and only those values of n work. In 1974 Roger Penrose found non-periodic tilings with five-fold symmetry, and in 1982 Dan Shechtman observed an alloy whose sharp diffraction spots showed five-fold and ten-fold symmetry, impossible for a periodic lattice; he was widely doubted before being vindicated, and received the 2011 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Dov Levine and Paul Steinhardt coined the word quasicrystal in 1984, and the number phi governs the spacing of their atomic planes. The counterpoint is about claims of beauty: the relation of phi to faces and paintings is much weaker than its tie to five-fold geometry, which is exact. This links to the nature door, where five-fold symmetry appears in flowers and starfish, which are alive and need not repeat."
