        "intuition": "Even in chaos, a natural balance emerges. Everyone adjusts until nobody needs to change. Order from freedom. In 1950 John Nash, then 21, described the idea in a short dissertation: a situation is in equilibrium when no player can do better by changing their own choice alone. It does not say the result is good. Take the prisoner's dilemma, formulated that year by Merrill Flood and Melvin Dresher. Two suspects are questioned apart. If both stay silent, each gets a light sentence, worth 3 points. If one betrays and the other is silent, the betrayer gets 5 and the silent one 0. If both betray, each gets 1. Whatever the other does, betraying pays more, so both betray, and each ends up with 1 when silence would have earned each 3. Mutual betrayal is the equilibrium, though both would prefer the other cell. Traffic does something similar. In 1968 Dietrich Braess showed in a model that adding a new road can make every driver's trip longer, because drivers' selfish choices settle into a worse equilibrium. Nash proved that every finite game has at least one equilibrium, if players may randomize. Order does emerge from freedom, but not always the order anyone would choose.",
