        "advanced": "Nash's theorem says every finite game has an equilibrium in mixed strategies. The proof applies a fixed-point theorem, in Nash's 1950 version Kakutani's 1941 generalization of Brouwer's theorem, to the best-response map. Antoine Cournot's 1838 analysis of competing firms had anticipated the idea for a special case. Nash shared the 1994 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economics with John Harsanyi and Reinhard Selten. John Maynard Smith and George Price's 1973 evolutionarily stable strategy is a refinement for populations, where no mutant can invade. The counterpoint is threefold. A game may have many equilibria, so the concept alone does not predict which will occur; equilibria can be inefficient, as in the prisoner's dilemma; and experiments, such as the ultimatum game, show people departing from predicted equilibria by caring about fairness. Equilibrium is a fixed point of mutual best response, not a moral ideal. This links to the nature door, where evolutionary game theory explains the proportion of aggressive and peaceful behaviors in animal populations."
