        "intuition": "You cannot have light without dark. They are partners, not enemies. Both sides are needed to turn on the light. The Chinese taijitu, the diagram of yin and yang, shows a circle divided by a curve into a dark and a light half, each holding a dot of the other. Mathematics has an exact version of the idea. Take any set A inside a universe U. Its complement is everything in U that is not in A. A and its complement share nothing, and together they make the whole, and neither can be defined without the other. Every number is either even or odd, never both, and each defines the other. The Chinese classic I Ching builds 64 figures called hexagrams from six lines, each either broken or unbroken, and 64 is 2 to the 6th. In 1703 Gottfried Leibniz, who had been sent the arrangement by the Jesuit Joachim Bouvet, recognized that the hexagrams, read as 0s and 1s, count from 0 to 63 in binary. Niels Bohr, who developed the principle of complementarity in 1927, held that a quantum object shows wave and particle aspects that exclude and yet complete each other. When he was knighted in 1947 he put a yin-yang symbol on his coat of arms with the motto contraria sunt complementa, opposites are complementary.",
