        "advanced": "The principle says that if P holds at 1 and P(n) implies P(n plus 1) for every n, then P holds for all natural numbers. It is one of the Peano axioms of 1889, following Richard Dedekind's 1888 analysis, and it is equivalent to the well-ordering principle that every nonempty set of natural numbers has a least element. Strong induction assumes P for all smaller values, and structural induction extends the method to trees and formulas. The counterpoint to its name is that mathematical induction is deductive, not inductive in the sense of generalizing from instances: the conclusion is guaranteed. The flawed horse argument shows what happens when the inductive step silently depends on a base case it does not cover. Induction can prove a formula once you have guessed it, but it does not find the formula. Finding it takes experiment and insight. This links to the philosophy door, where Henri Poincare argued that induction is the characteristic mathematical reasoning, a kind of intuition of the infinite."
