        "intuition": "Agree on the simplest truths first, and a whole world of knowledge can be built on top. The base determines the building. An axiomatic system starts from a short list of statements accepted without proof and a set of rules for deriving more. Euclid did it for geometry around 300 BC, and the idea matured in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. The group axioms need only four lines: combining stays inside, grouping does not matter, there is a do-nothing element, and every element can be undone. From these four, an entire theory of symmetry follows. Giuseppe Peano in 1889 described the whole of ordinary arithmetic with five axioms about a first number and its successors. Three questions are asked of a system. Is it consistent, meaning no contradiction can be derived? Are the axioms independent, meaning none follows from the others? Is it complete, meaning every statement can be settled? The surprise is that some questions are independent of the standard foundations. The axiom of choice, formulated by Ernst Zermelo in 1904, cannot be disproved from the other axioms of set theory, as Kurt Godel showed in 1938, and cannot be proved from them, as Paul Cohen showed in 1963. Cohen showed the same for the continuum hypothesis. Choosing an axiom is a choice of world.",
