        "advanced": "A formal system has a language, axioms and inference rules; a model is a structure in which the axioms hold. David Hilbert's 1899 Foundations of Geometry made independence proofs routine: to show an axiom is independent, build a model where it fails, as Eugenio Beltrami did for the parallel postulate in 1868. Hilbert's program of the 1920s sought a finitary proof that mathematics is consistent, and Godel's 1931 theorems showed this cannot succeed for systems containing arithmetic. The counterpoint is that foundations are plural. Luitzen Brouwer's intuitionism rejects the unrestricted law of the excluded middle, and category theory, begun by Samuel Eilenberg and Saunders Mac Lane in 1945, offers a different starting point. Most working mathematicians use the axioms without thinking about them, and the theorems they prove remain valid in any system that satisfies those axioms. This links to the philosophy door, where the status of the axioms, truth or convention, is the oldest argument in the subject."
