        "intuition": "Big fact plus small fact equals new truth. The conclusion was always there, hiding in the premises. Three steps to certainty. Aristotle founded the study of syllogisms in the Prior Analytics in the fourth century BC. Each has two premises and a conclusion built from three terms, and the form named Barbara runs: all M are P, all S are M, so all S are P. Substitute: all mammals are warm-blooded, all dogs are mammals, therefore all dogs are warm-blooded. You can check validity with a picture. John Venn's diagrams of 1880 draw overlapping circles and shade the regions that the premises rule out, and then you simply see whether the conclusion is left standing. Not every pattern works. All dogs are animals, all cats are animals, so all dogs are cats. This has the shape of a syllogism and is false, because being inside the same large circle does not put two small circles inside each other. The middle term, animals, was never used to connect the other two. Counting all arrangements of the three kinds of statement across the four possible figures gives 256 possible forms, and only 24 of them are valid. Medieval students memorized names like Barbara and Celarent to hold the good ones. Certainty is rare, and it has a pattern.",
