        "advanced": "A categorical syllogism's validity depends on the distribution of the middle term: it must be universally covered in at least one premise, or the argument commits the fallacy of the undistributed middle. George Boole's 1847 Mathematical Analysis of Logic and Augustus De Morgan's work turned syllogistic reasoning into algebra, and Gottlob Frege's quantifiers in 1879 absorbed it as a small fragment of a stronger system. The counterpoint is its narrowness. De Morgan pointed out that the syllogism cannot establish that if all horses are animals, then the head of a horse is the head of an animal, since this needs reasoning about relations. Most mathematical argument involves relations and many quantifiers, which is where Frege's logic took over, and for two thousand years the syllogism was thought to be nearly all of logic. A limited tool taken for the whole is a lesson of its own. This links to the philosophy door, where Aristotle's logic shaped medieval thought for centuries."
