        "intuition": "The most complex things are built from simple yes-or-no choices. AND, OR, NOT: three gates to build a universe. In 1854 George Boole published An Investigation of the Laws of Thought and showed that logic can be done like algebra with just two values, true and false, or 1 and 0. AND gives 1 only when both inputs are 1. OR gives 1 when at least one is. NOT flips. De Morgan's laws connect them: not (A and B) is the same as (not A) or (not B). Eighty years later, in 1937, the young Claude Shannon noticed in his master's thesis that electrical switches in series behave like AND and in parallel like OR, and that logic could therefore be built from circuits. Now watch arithmetic appear. Add two bits. The sum bit is 1 when exactly one input is 1, called exclusive or, and the carry bit is 1 only when both are, which is AND. So 1 plus 1 gives sum 0 and carry 1, which reads 10, the number 2. Chain these together and you get a machine that adds any numbers. Even one gate is enough: in 1913 Henry Sheffer showed that NAND alone, not-and, can build all the others. A few gates, wired together, add, remember and decide.",
