        "advanced": "A Boolean algebra is a set with operations meet, join and complement obeying the usual laws; the subsets of any set form one, so Boole's algebra and the algebra of sets are the same. For n inputs there are 2 to the 2 to the n Boolean functions, so 16 for two inputs, and every one can be written using AND, OR and NOT. Deciding whether a Boolean formula can be made true, the satisfiability problem, was proved by Stephen Cook in 1971 to be NP-complete, the first problem so shown, and whether it can be solved quickly is the open P versus NP question. The counterpoint is that two-valued logic describes the switches, not the world, which is why fuzzy logic and probability extend it for partial truth. Boole himself thought he was describing the laws of thought, a claim that is now seen as optimistic. This links to the consciousness door, where the question is whether thinking is computation."
