        "intuition": "Strip away the words. Look at the skeleton. If the structure holds, the truth holds. Form over content. Propositional logic uses letters, P and Q, for whole statements, and a few connectives to join them: not, and, or, and if-then. Each statement is true or false, and a truth table lists every case. With 2 statements there are 4 rows, and with 3 there are 8. Most connectives match everyday speech, but if-then is trickier. P implies Q is false in exactly one case: when P is true and Q is false. Think of a promise: if it rains, I will bring an umbrella. It is broken only if it rains and I do not. If it does not rain, the promise has not been broken, whatever I carry. This explains two forms people often confuse. The contrapositive, if not Q then not P, is exactly equivalent to the original: if I had no umbrella, it did not rain. The converse, if Q then P, is not: seeing an umbrella does not mean it rained. A formula that is true in every row is a tautology, such as P or not P. The Stoic philosopher Chrysippus developed such logic in the third century BC. Seeing the skeleton can save you from arguments that merely sound right.",
