        "advanced": "The syntax of propositional logic defines well-formed formulas, and the semantics assigns truth values to atoms and computes the rest. Emil Post proved in 1921 that the system is complete: every tautology has a proof, and Wittgenstein's Tractatus of the same year popularized truth tables. Whether a formula is a tautology can be decided by a table of 2 to the n rows, which is why propositional logic is decidable, though the best known general methods stay exponential, in line with Cook's 1971 result on satisfiability. Gottlob Frege's 1879 system extended the idea to quantifiers. The counterpoint concerns the material conditional. A false statement implies anything, so if the Moon is cheese then 2 plus 2 is 5 is true, and this clashes with how if-then is used in speech, where we expect a connection between the parts. Philosophers such as C. I. Lewis introduced stricter conditionals for this reason, and relevance logics followed. This links to the philosophy door, where the meaning of if-then remains disputed."
