        "intuition": "The universe is always a little bigger than our brains. Some truths are real but unprovable. The net has holes, but the ocean is still there. In 1931 Kurt Godel, aged 25, proved that any consistent system of axioms powerful enough to do arithmetic, with a rule for recognizing the axioms, contains statements that are true but cannot be proved within it. His method was an astonishing trick. He assigned every statement a unique number, so that statements about numbers could talk about statements. Then he built a sentence that says, in effect, this sentence cannot be proved in the system. If the system proved it, it would be proving something false, and the system would be broken. So it cannot prove it, and then what the sentence says is true. A true statement sits outside the system's reach. Add that statement as a new axiom and the trick can be played again on the bigger system. Godel's second theorem adds that such a system cannot prove its own consistency. Notice what the theorem does not say. Ordinary mathematics is not riddled with holes. Natural examples of unprovable statements, such as Goodstein's theorem, proved unprovable in standard arithmetic by Laurence Kirby and Jeff Paris in 1982, are rare and technical. The net has holes, but the sea of provable truth is vast.",
