        "intuition": "Assume the opposite. Watch it explode. Therefore your original claim stands. Prove truth by disproving its shadow. One of the great examples is Georg Cantor's 1891 proof that the real numbers cannot be listed. Suppose, for contradiction, that someone has a complete list of all the numbers between 0 and 1, written as decimals. Build a new number by going down the list: make its first digit different from the first digit of the first number, its second digit different from the second digit of the second number, and so on, always choosing digits other than 0 or 9 to avoid tricky double names. The new number differs from every number on the list in at least one place, so it is not on the list, even though the list was said to be complete. The assumption has led to a contradiction, so it must be false. No list can hold them all. There are, in a precise sense, more real numbers than counting numbers, and the infinite comes in different sizes. G. H. Hardy called this style of argument one of a mathematician's finest weapons, a gambit far finer than any in chess. The mathematician does not sacrifice a pawn, he said, but offers the whole game. A claim is proved by showing that its denial cannot live.",
