        "advanced": "Reductio ad absurdum proves P by deriving a contradiction from not-P, relying on the classical law that not-not-P implies P. A neat instance of its non-constructive character: let a be the square root of 2 to the power the square root of 2. If a is rational, we have an irrational power of an irrational number that is rational. If not, a to the square root of 2 equals 2, which is rational. Either way such a pair exists, yet the argument does not say which case holds. The counterpoint is the intuitionism of L. E. J. Brouwer, who in the 1920s rejected double-negation elimination for infinite domains and insisted that to prove something exists one must exhibit it, a program Errett Bishop developed in 1967 in constructive analysis. A proof by contradiction shows what must be, and not always how to find it. This links to the philosophy door, where the debate over indirect proof is a debate over what existence means."
