        "intuition": "If nothing is written, everything is permitted. Not as a threat. As an invitation. The universe gave you a blank page. Nihilism is the claim that the universe has no built-in purpose, and for many people that sounds like despair. But Albert Camus saw a way through. In The Myth of Sisyphus he looked at the king condemned by the gods to roll a stone up a hill forever, only to watch it roll back down. This looks like the picture of meaningless toil. Camus concluded that one must imagine Sisyphus happy. The moment he walks back down the hill, knowing his fate and not fooled by hope, he is above it. The rock is his. The philosopher Thomas Nagel, in 1971, made a related point with a dry smile: if nothing matters from the point of view of the universe, then that fact does not matter either. The cosmic view is only one view, and no more authoritative than your own. Permitted does not mean careless. It means the weight is yours to carry. If no one will tell you what your life is for, then what you give your days to is, entirely, your choice.",
        "links": [
          {
            "label": "Nihilism",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nihilism"
          },
          {
            "label": "Absurdism",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absurdism"
          }
        ],
        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "You've Got a Friend in Me",
            "artist": "Randy Newman"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Philosophers distinguish several nihilisms: existential nihilism, that life has no objective meaning, moral nihilism, that there are no moral facts, and epistemic nihilism, that knowledge is impossible. Optimistic nihilism is a popular label for the first view joined to an upbeat conclusion, and the philosophical ancestry runs through Nietzsche's announcement of the death of God, Sartre's freedom and Camus's revolt against the absurd. Nagel's essay The Absurd argued that the human predicament is absurd because we take our lives seriously while knowing we could doubt that seriousness, and that irony, not heroism or despair, is the fitting response. Susan Wolf's account of meaning in life rejects the stark choice: meaning arises when subjective engagement meets objectively worthwhile activity. The central objection to the optimistic view is that freedom from a given purpose does not produce a reason to prefer one choice to another, and critics call it a constructivism wearing a nihilist's coat. The long-term fate of the universe, in cosmology, is the door of science."
