        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Spectrum",
            "artist": "Florence + The Machine"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "The social nature of humans was Aristotle's starting point when he called man a political animal, and Durkheim's 1897 study Suicide showed statistically that rates varied with the degree of social integration. Robin Dunbar proposed that the size of the primate neocortex limits the number of stable relationships, giving roughly one hundred and fifty for humans, a figure that is a rough estimate. The Harvard study, directed in later years by Robert Waldinger, and a large meta-analysis by Julianne Holt-Lunstad in 2010 both link strong social ties to longer life, though the studies are observational and loneliness may sometimes follow illness. The African philosophy of ubuntu, summed up as a person is a person through other persons, makes relation the ground of identity, not a feature of it. A caution applies: connection can mean conformity and pressure as much as support, and solitude has its own value. The binding force behind it all is the subject of the door of love."
