        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Small World",
            "artist": "Idina Menzel"
          },
          {
            "title": "You've Got a Friend",
            "artist": "James Taylor"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Amor fati appears in The Gay Science, section 276, and Nietzsche later called it his formula for greatness in a human being in Ecce Homo: wanting nothing to be different, not forward, not backward, not in all eternity. It is tied to the doctrine of eternal recurrence, which he posed in section 341 as a test of one's affirmation of life. The Stoics held a close cousin of the view: Epictetus and Marcus Aurelius counselled that we should want events to happen as they do and not as we would like, since we control only our judgments. The difference is that the Stoics grounded acceptance in a rational cosmos, while Nietzsche grounded it in strength of will, without a cosmic order to rely on. The standard objection is that the attitude seems to counsel passivity or approve of terrible things, but Nietzsche meant affirmation of one's own life, which is compatible with working hard to change what lies ahead. Love that says yes to the whole is a theme of the door of mysticism."
