        "intuition": "Two truths walk into a room. A third truth walks out. That is a syllogism. The conclusion was hiding inside the premises. Aristotle noticed the pattern while studying how people argue, and wrote it down in a work called the Prior Analytics. The most famous form goes: all humans are mortal, all Greeks are humans, so all Greeks are mortal. Picture it as circles. Draw a large circle for mortals, and inside it a smaller one for humans. Greeks sit inside humans, so they must sit inside mortals. In the 1880s John Venn made the picture standard, and anyone can use it now to test an argument. Try a false one. All dogs are animals, all cats are animals, therefore all dogs are cats. Draw it. Dogs are inside animals, cats are inside animals, but nothing forces the two small circles to overlap, let alone become one. The argument looks tidy and breaks as soon as you draw it. That is the value of the old form. It slows you down enough to see what is actually being claimed, and whether the conclusion has earned its place.",
        "links": [
          {
            "label": "Syllogism Examples",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syllogism"
          },
          {
            "label": "Term logic",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Term_logic"
          }
        ],
        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "In Case You Don't Live Forever",
            "artist": "Ben Platt"
          },
          {
            "title": "World Spins Madly On",
            "artist": "The Weepies"
          },
          {
            "title": "Constant Craving",
            "artist": "k.d. lang"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Aristotle's syllogism has two premises and a conclusion built from four kinds of statement: all A are B, no A are B, some A are B, some A are not B. Combining them in the three possible figures, and a fourth added later, gives 256 forms, of which 24 are valid in the traditional system, nine of them only if one assumes the terms are not empty. Medieval students memorized them through mnemonic names, such as Barbara for the first and most famous. Kant remarked that logic had not needed to take a step since Aristotle, a claim overturned when Frege created predicate logic. The old system has a real limit: it cannot handle relations. Augustus De Morgan's example shows it. From the premise that every horse is an animal, we would like to infer that the head of a horse is the head of an animal, and no syllogism can show it. The patterns of valid inference still support the door of mathematics."
