        "intuition": "We do not invent math. We discover it. The circle was perfect before anyone drew one. The map was there before the explorers. Here is a thought to test it. About 300 BCE Euclid proved that the prime numbers never run out. Whatever list of primes you hold, multiply them all together and add one, and you get a number that none of them divides, so either it is prime or it has a prime factor missing from your list. The proof is short and no one has ever found a flaw. Now imagine intelligent beings on a distant world, with a different biology, a different language, and no contact with us. If they studied numbers, would they find the same result? It seems they would, and that is the intuition behind Platonism. No one voted for the infinity of primes. It was not decided by the structure of our brains. It seems to be something we ran into, the way an explorer meets a mountain. The strange part is where it lives. Numbers have no weight, no color and no place. If they are real, they are real in a way that nothing else is. The puzzle of math is that it feels discovered, though what it is discovered in remains a mystery.",
