        "intuition": "You cannot live in yesterday. You cannot live in tomorrow. You can only live in this breath. This is the only moment that has ever been. Yet the present is harder to find than it seems. Augustine, in the fourth century, worked it out in the Confessions. A year is not present, because most of it is past or to come. A month, the same. A day, an hour, the same, since only a fraction of each is happening. Divide as far as you like and the present shrinks toward a point with no length at all, which is gone before it can be grasped. How can we speak of something so thin? Yet we experience a moment with some width. The psychologist William James, borrowing a term from another writer, called it the specious present: a few seconds in which a melody is heard as a melody and not as a series of notes, and a sentence as a sentence. The now is not a razor edge. It is a short window where the just-past still echoes and the next moment is already leaning in. That is why attention matters. It is the only access you have to anything. The past is memory and the future is guess, and only the window in which you are reading is real to you.",
