        "advanced": "Presentism holds that only the present exists, eternalism that past, present and future are equally real, and the growing-block view that past and present exist but the future does not. J. M. E. McTaggart's 1908 paper argued that time requires the A-series, events as past, present and future, and that the A-series is contradictory, so time is unreal. Special relativity pressures presentism: because simultaneity depends on the observer's motion, there seems to be no single universal present, a point pressed in the Putnam-Rietdijk argument. Phenomenology approaches the question from within: Husserl described the present as structured by retention of what just passed and protention of what comes next, which fits James's specious present. Augustine's puzzle remains the clearest statement: the present has no extent, yet it is the only thing we ever live in. Some defenders of presentism answer that relativity is a theory of measurement and not of what exists. The disciplines of attention to the present moment are old in the door of mysticism."
