        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "It's Ok (Acoustic)",
            "artist": "Tom Rosenthal"
          },
          {
            "title": "Immigrant Song",
            "artist": "Led Zeppelin"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Aristotle distinguished four kinds of cause: material, formal, efficient and final. Modern debate centers on the efficient cause. Hume's analysis reduces it to constant conjunction, and Kant answered that causality is a category the mind imposes, making experience of an ordered world possible. David Lewis's 1973 account defines causation by counterfactuals: A caused B if B would not have occurred had A not occurred. Judea Pearl and others developed a formal theory of interventions, which asks what would happen if we set a variable by force, and gives a precise basis for the maxim that correlation is not causation. Bertrand Russell argued in 1913 that the fundamental laws of physics contain no notion of cause at all, only equations relating states. In relativity, causes are limited to the light cone: no influence travels faster than light, and quantum entanglement does not allow signalling. The sciences that find causes are the subject of the door of science."
