        "advanced": "Impermanence is one of the three marks of existence in Buddhism, along with dukkha, unsatisfactoriness, and anatta, not-self. Early Buddhist schools such as the Sarvastivada and Sautrantika developed this into the doctrine of momentariness, in which phenomena exist only for an instant and continuity is a stream of successive events. Heraclitus's fragments, preserved by Plato and others, describe a world in flux held together by a unifying logos, and Plato's Forms were in part an answer to flux, seeking changeless objects of knowledge, while Parmenides had denied change altogether. The puzzle of identity through change appears in the Ship of Theseus, and Derek Parfit argued that personal identity is less important than psychological continuity. Modern physics supports a version of the view in that bodies are processes with turnover of matter, though a precise figure for how much is replaced depends on the tissue. The risk of the teaching, critics note, is detachment as avoidance. To know change and rest in it is what the door of mysticism points to."
