      {
        "id": "arrow-of-time",
        "num": 10,
        "icon": "🏹",
        "title": "The Arrow of Time",
        "subtitle": "The One-Way Street",
        "simple": "Imagine a movie of someone jumping into a pool. Play it forward: they splash in. Play it backward: they fly out and land on the diving board! In real life, we only see the forward version. This one-way direction is the Arrow of Time. Even though some math says time could go both ways, the universe has a strict rule that we only move toward the future. It is a one-way street we are all walking down together.",
        "senses": [
          {
            "key": "hear",
            "icon": "👂",
            "sense": "SOUND",
            "text": "A glass breaking and never un-breaking."
          },
          {
            "key": "smell",
            "icon": "👃",
            "sense": "SMELL",
            "text": "Toast: you cannot turn it back into bread."
          },
          {
            "key": "taste",
            "icon": "👅",
            "sense": "TASTE",
            "text": "A mixed smoothie: you cannot un-mix the fruit."
          },
          {
            "key": "touch",
            "icon": "✋",
            "sense": "TOUCH",
            "text": "Heat leaving a warm cup of cocoa."
          },
          {
            "key": "see",
            "icon": "👁️",
            "sense": "SIGHT",
            "text": "Smoke rising from a blown-out candle."
          },
          {
            "key": "body",
            "icon": "🫀",
            "sense": "BODY",
            "text": "The feeling of moving forward as you walk: your body on the one-way street."
          }
        ],
        "intuition": "You cannot un-burn the toast. You cannot un-break the glass. The arrow flies forward. And that is not a curse. Play a film of a glass falling off a table and smashing, then play it backward. You know at once which is real. But the strange fact is that the basic laws of motion do not care. A single collision between two billiard balls looks equally believable run forward or backward. Newton's laws, Maxwell's equations and the equations of quantum mechanics are almost entirely symmetric in time. So where does the arrow come from? Boltzmann's answer was statistical. There are vastly more ways for things to be messy than tidy, so a system left alone drifts toward the messy, and we call that direction the future. This raises a deeper question: why was the past tidy to begin with? The early universe, just after the Big Bang, was in an extraordinarily smooth, low-entropy state, and everything since has been running downhill from there. Our memory works only in this direction too, because making a record is itself an increase of disorder somewhere. We remember the past and not the future for the same reason eggs break and do not un-break. Time flows because the universe began well-ordered.",
        "links": [
          {
            "label": "Arrow of time",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrow_of_time"
          },
          {
            "label": "Entropy",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entropy"
          }
        ],
        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Always on My Mind",
            "artist": "Willie Nelson"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "The phrase arrow of time was coined by Arthur Eddington in 1927. Several arrows are distinguished: thermodynamic, the growth of entropy; cosmological, the expansion of the universe; radiative, waves spreading outward and not inward; and psychological, memory of the past but not the future. The puzzle is that the fundamental laws are nearly time-symmetric, as Loschmidt noted in 1876 in his reversibility objection to Boltzmann. The standard resolution, developed by Boltzmann and, in modern form, by Penrose, Albert and Carroll, is the Past Hypothesis: the universe began in a state of very low entropy, which the laws alone do not explain. The weak interaction violates time-reversal symmetry slightly, as found in kaon decay by Cronin and Fitch in 1964, but this is not thought to account for the everyday arrow. Whether the low-entropy beginning has a deeper explanation, as in cyclic or inflationary models, is an open question. The question of why we remember what has been belongs to the door of consciousness."
      }
