        "intuition": "The vase broke. But the gold that fills the crack makes it stronger and more beautiful than before. Healing is not forgetting. It is gilding the scars. In Japan the craft of kintsugi repairs broken pottery with lacquer mixed with gold, so that the cracks become the most visible part. Restorative justice asks the same of a wrong. The usual question of the courtroom is what law was broken and what punishment the offender deserves. Howard Zehr, whose book Changing Lenses in 1990 gave the movement its voice, proposed three different questions: who has been hurt, what do they need, and whose job is it to put things right? The harm is the center, and the people involved are brought into the room. A young person who broke into a house might hear from the family who lived there what it meant to them, listen, answer, and agree on how to repair what was done. Some of the first such programs began in Ontario in 1974, among Mennonites. New Zealand built conferences into its youth justice law in 1989, drawing on Maori practice. Many victims say that what they most wanted was an answer, an apology and to be heard. Restorative justice does not excuse harm. It asks the one who did it to face it, and offers a way back.",
