        "intuition": "The ice held the water. The water held the story. When it let go, the whole coastline disappeared. And people remembered. At the peak of the last Ice Age, about twenty thousand years ago, so much water was locked in the great ice sheets that the sea stood roughly 120 meters lower than today. Where there is now the North Sea, there was a plain with rivers and hunters, which archaeologists call Doggerland. The ice did not melt slowly and evenly. There were surges. Geologists identify a rapid rise known as Meltwater Pulse 1A, around fourteen and a half thousand years ago, when the sea rose several meters in a few centuries. A coastline that moved inland by a few meters in a lifetime would be noticed. Grandparents would say the sea used to be farther away. In Australia, Aboriginal stories from many coastal regions describe lands that were once dry and are now under water, and researchers have argued that their details match the sea level as it was more than seven thousand years ago. If so, a story can ride seven thousand years in the mouths of people. The water rose. The people moved. The telling was how they stayed who they were.",
        "links": [
          {
            "label": "Nature: Meltwater Pulse 1A",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meltwater_pulse_1A"
          },
          {
            "label": "Sea level rise",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_level_rise"
          }
        ],
        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Hell Without the Flames",
            "artist": "Josiah and the Bonnevilles"
          },
          {
            "title": "Lake of Fire (Live Acoustic)",
            "artist": "Nirvana"
          },
          {
            "title": "The Gambler",
            "artist": "Kenny Rogers"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Sea level rose by roughly 120 meters between the Last Glacial Maximum, about 20,000 years ago, and around 7,000 years ago, driven by the melting of the Laurentide, Fennoscandian and Antarctic ice sheets. Records from drowned coral reefs, notably off Barbados and Tahiti, show abrupt steps called meltwater pulses, including Meltwater Pulse 1A near 14,600 years ago and a smaller 1B near 11,500, though the size and source of 1A remain debated. Doggerland in the North Sea and the Sunda shelf of Southeast Asia were once habitable land. In 2016 the geographers Patrick Nunn and Nicholas Reid argued that Aboriginal Australian stories of coastal inundation preserve memory of sea level changes over 7,000 years old. The strongest counterpoint is that dating a story is hard: a tale may be reshaped many times, and a match to a date can be coincidence. Nothing here shows a single global flood. It connects to the science door in climate and glaciology, and the nature door in the fact that coasts are borrowed."
