        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "American Beauty (OurVinyl Sessions)",
            "artist": "Drew Holcomb"
          },
          {
            "title": "The Alcott (feat. Taylor Swift)",
            "artist": "The National"
          },
          {
            "title": "Keep on Pushing",
            "artist": "The Impressions"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "The flood section of Gilgamesh belongs to the Standard Babylonian version, usually attributed to the scribe Sin-leqi-unninni and dated to around 1200 BCE, but it draws on older material, notably the Old Babylonian Atrahasis epic of about 1700 BCE and Sumerian flood stories of the Ziusudra tradition. The tablets were found in Ashurbanipal's library at Nineveh, and George Smith announced the flood tablet to the Society of Biblical Archaeology in December 1872. The parallels with Genesis are close: the boat, the birds, the mountain landing, the offering. Most scholars hold that the biblical authors drew on Mesopotamian tradition, perhaps during or after the exile in Babylon. The strongest counterpoint concerns purpose: in Atrahasis the gods send the flood because humans are too noisy, a very different moral logic from Genesis. The epic joins the religion door through its link with Genesis and the philosophy door through its theme of mortality."
