        "intuition": "Nothing is ever lost. Not a word. Not a thought. Not a feeling. The library is always open. And your story is on the shelf next to everyone else's. The word akasha is Sanskrit for space or ether, the subtle fifth element in which sound travels in classical Indian thought. The idea of a record written in it is much newer. It was shaped in the late nineteenth century by the Theosophists, notably Helena Blavatsky and A. P. Sinnett, and developed by Rudolf Steiner, who wrote of an Akasha Chronicle that a trained seer could read, and later popularized by the American healer Edgar Cayce. Is there any truth in the picture? There is a physical echo, though a small one. Light from events keeps traveling outward forever, so that somewhere in space tonight there are photons carrying the sight of your birth, and they will never fully fade. And physics holds that information is conserved in principle: a burned book, in theory, could be reconstructed from the smoke and ash if you could track every particle. In practice nothing can. The records, if they exist, are scrambled past recovery. But the longing behind the idea is honest. We want to believe that a life leaves a mark that cannot be erased.",
