        "advanced": "Akasha in Sanskrit texts is one of the five great elements, linked to space and sound, and appears in the Upanishads and Samkhya. The notion of a cosmic memory called the Akashic Records is a modern development: it appears in the Theosophical writings of Blavatsky and Sinnett in the 1880s, in Steiner's Akasha Chronicle of the early twentieth century, and in the trance readings of Edgar Cayce, and is not attested as such in classical Indian sources. There is no empirical evidence that the records can be read. The physical principle of unitarity, which holds that quantum information is not destroyed, and the debate over information loss in black holes associated with Stephen Hawking, give a loose analogy but not support. The strongest counterpoint is that the claims are untestable, and that readings by psychics have never been verified. It connects to the mysticism door in the idea of a universal memory and the science door in information theory."
