        "advanced": "The phrase wood wide web was coined by David Read in a 1997 Nature commentary on Simard and colleagues' paper showing carbon transfer between birch and Douglas fir through mycorrhizal links. Forest ecology since has described common mycorrhizal networks, hub trees and signal transfer, and the idea of the forest as a superorganism, pictured by some as self-regulating in the manner of James Lovelock and Lynn Margulis's Gaia hypothesis. The strong superorganism claim is not supported, because natural selection acts on individual trees, whose interests only partly overlap; the scientific consensus holds that mycorrhizal networks exist and matter, but the extent of directed sharing is unresolved, as Karst and colleagues argued in 2023. Facilitation and competition coexist, and the balance varies with species, soil and conditions. The cleaner finding is of interdependence: nearly all trees need fungal partners. This card meets the door of Mythology: Yggdrasil, the world tree of Norse myth, joins realms through its roots, and the soil shows a living version of that image."
