        "intuition": "Light does not defeat darkness. Light needs darkness. Without the night, the stars would have no stage. The stars are up there all day, shining as fiercely as ever. You cannot see them because our own sky, lit by the sun and scattered by the air, drowns them out. They appear only when the sun goes away. That is more than a poetic point. In 1823 the German astronomer Heinrich Olbers asked why the night sky is dark at all. If the universe were endless, static and eternal, every line of sight would end on the surface of a star, and the whole sky would blaze as bright as the sun. The sky is dark because the universe has a beginning, and light from the farthest regions has not had time to arrive. The darkness of the night is evidence about the history of everything. The old Chinese words yin and yang started from the same idea. Yin first meant the shaded side of a hill, and yang the sunny side. They are not two hills. They are one hill seen at different hours, and the shade moves as the sun does. A front cannot be cut from its back, and a hill cannot lose its shady slope. What you think of as opposites are often two ends of one thing.",
