        "intuition": "Finding the perfect wiggle is like finding the perfect heartbeat. Vibrato is a slight, regular variation in the pitch of a sustained note, usually around five to seven cycles a second in a trained singer, wide enough to give warmth without sounding wobbly. A straight tone can sound cold. A very wide, slow wobble sounds uncontrolled. Vibrato in the right measure sounds alive. It also helps a voice be heard over an orchestra, and it makes slight tuning errors less obvious, because the ear averages the pitch. A natural vibrato arises when the throat and breath are free. Singers who force it often make it worse, and teachers say you cannot build vibrato by shaking, you can only remove what stops it. Voices also use it expressively, adding it late in a held note to make it bloom, or leaving it out to sound plain, haunted or very young. Early-music singers often use less of it than opera singers, for reasons of style. Vibrato is not something you add to a note. It is what a note does when it is alive and has room to move.",
