        "advanced": "Vibrato is a periodic modulation of pitch and, to a lesser extent, of loudness, with a typical rate in trained Western classical singers of about five to seven hertz and an extent of roughly half a semitone either side of the center. Carl Seashore's studies at Iowa in the 1930s measured it in detail. Its origin is debated: one account sees it as a natural oscillation in the neuromuscular feedback loops that control the laryngeal muscles, another as a by-product of stable, relaxed phonation. Listeners hear a vibrato tone as a single pitch near the average. The idea that vibrato should be continuous is historical: in the twentieth century, as recording and large halls favoured it, it became standard in orchestras, while early-music scholars argue that eighteenth-century violinists treated it as an ornament, as Leopold Mozart's 1756 violin treatise suggests. Vibrato ties song to the doors of the body and nature: a living tone is never perfectly still."
