        "intuition": "Your timbre is your fingerprint in the air. You can copy someone's melody. You can copy their rhythm and their words. But you cannot fully copy their timbre. It is shaped by the length and thickness of your vocal folds, the size and shape of your throat, mouth, nose and sinuses, the way your tongue sits, and even the habits you picked up from the voices you heard as a child. Impressionists do not reproduce a voice exactly. They catch the handful of features the ear uses to recognise a person and exaggerate them. Voice analysis programs can tell speakers apart from a few seconds of speech, which is why voiceprints are used as identity checks, though they can be fooled. A singer also has several timbres of their own: bright, dark, breathy, a belt, a gentle falsetto, and choosing among them is like choosing a color to paint with. Many singers became famous not because their voice was the most beautiful but because it was unmistakable. Everything else about a song can be shared. Your timbre is the one thing that belongs only to you.",
        "links": [
          {
            "label": "Timbre",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timbre"
          },
          {
            "label": "Harmonic Series",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmonic_series_(music)"
          },
          {
            "label": "Auditory Scene Analysis",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auditory_scene_analysis"
          }
        ],
        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Redemption Song",
            "artist": "Johnny Cash"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Voice timbre is a product of the source and the tract. The source, the glottal waveform, varies with vocal fold mass, tension and closure, producing registers such as modal, falsetto and creaky voice. The tract, which averages around seventeen centimetres in adult men and is shorter in women and children, filters the source through its formants. Because tract dimensions are partly set by anatomy, formant frequencies carry information about body size and identity, and listeners use them to judge speakers. Performers can alter timbre deliberately: teachers describe qualities such as covered and open, or twang, a bright quality associated with narrowing the space above the larynx, studied by Jo Estill and by voice scientists. The limit is that timbre depends on the room and the recording chain as well as the singer, and voices change with age, health and mood. Timbre ties song to the door of consciousness: a voice is a self you can hear."
