        "advanced": "Beat perception engages a network linking the auditory cortex with premotor cortex, the supplementary motor area and the basal ganglia. Jessica Grahn and Matthew Brett showed in 2007 that the basal ganglia respond more strongly to rhythms with a clear beat, and people with Parkinson's disease, in whom this circuitry is damaged, show poorer beat perception. Neural resonance theory, advanced by Edward Large, proposes that populations of neurons oscillate and lock onto the pulse of music. Synchronised movement has been found to increase cooperation and bonding, effects studied by Bronwyn Tarr, Jacques Launay and Robin Dunbar. William McNeill, in Keeping Together in Time (1995), suggested that muscular bonding through drill and dance has helped build human communities, an influential but speculative thesis. A limit is that experimental effects are often small, and bonding may depend on context and on the choice to take part. The topic connects dance to the doors of nature and religion, where processions and circle dances have long made one body of many."
