        "intuition": "There is always a peaceful space in the middle to help you adjust. In Tibetan Buddhism the bardo, which means in-between, is the passage between one life and the next. The Bardo Thodol, known in the West as the Tibetan Book of the Dead, is read aloud to the dying and the newly dead and is traditionally understood to guide the mind through a period of forty-nine days. The teaching is subtle. It says that at the moment of death, the mind meets a clear radiance, the nature of mind itself, and that if the person can recognize it as their own awareness, liberation is possible. If not, the mind meets visions, peaceful ones and fierce ones, and the instruction is always the same: these are projections of your own mind, do not be afraid, and recognize them. Notice that this is a practical manual, not a poem. It teaches that the way to meet death is to have practiced meeting your own mind in life, which is why Tibetan teachers speak of bardos in daily life too: the gap between waking and sleep, between one thought and the next. Whether or not one accepts the picture of rebirth, the teaching offers something every person can use. Fear shrinks when you see what it is made of.",
        "links": [
          {
            "label": "Bardo",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bardo"
          },
          {
            "label": "Liminality",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liminality"
          },
          {
            "label": "Purgatory",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purgatory"
          }
        ],
        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Holy, Holy, Holy",
            "artist": "Traditional Hymn"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Bardo (Tibetan, the between) names states between death and rebirth, and in the Nyingma teachings also the intervals of living, dreaming, meditation, dying, the luminosity of dharmata, and becoming. The Bardo Thodol (Liberation Through Hearing in the Intermediate State) is a text of the Nyingma tradition attributed to the tradition of Padmasambhava and revealed by Karma Lingpa in the fourteenth century; it is read to the dying and deceased over a traditional forty-nine days. The text teaches recognition of the clear light at death and of the peaceful and wrathful appearances as manifestations of one's own mind. The 1927 Evans-Wentz translation, which introduced it to the West, framed it with ideas from Theosophy that scholars have criticized. Buddhist schools differ: Theravada generally does not teach an intermediate state, while Tibetan and some East Asian schools do. The limit is that its claims are doctrinal, accepted through practice and trust. It meets mysticism and the psychology of fear."
