        "intuition": "You are standing on holy ground wherever you are. To make a place holy, almost every tradition does the same first thing: it marks a boundary. At the burning bush, a voice tells Moses to take off his sandals, for the place where he stands is holy ground. Jacob wakes from a dream at a stony place and says, surely the Lord is in this place and I did not know it. Muslims fix their prayer toward Mecca, and the Prophet is recorded as saying that the earth has been made a place of prayer. Hindus walk to the tirthas, river crossings where the worlds are thought to touch. Japanese Shinto marks a sacred spot with a gate and a rope. The scholar Mircea Eliade argued that sacred space is not like ordinary space: it has a center, a place where heaven and earth meet. The ritual act of marking, entering, washing the hands, covering the head or taking off the shoes does something to the one who does it. It tells the mind that what comes next is not ordinary. Notice also that the traditions keep arguing with themselves. The prophets said that God does not live in houses built by hands. A holy place can be built. But it can also be made, by the person who walks in with attention.",
