        "intuition": "Using real things makes us feel real too. Sacred builders have always cared what a place is made of. The Bible says that stones for the altar must be whole, not cut with an iron tool, and that the Temple was built with stones prepared at the quarry so that no hammer was heard in the house. Cathedrals are carved from local stone that took generations to haul. Purity rules go with them. Muslims perform wudu, washing hands, face and feet, before prayer. Jews immerse in the mikveh. Shinto worshippers rinse their hands and mouth at the temple fountain, and Hindus bathe at the Ganges. The anthropologist Mary Douglas, in Purity and Danger, argued that ideas of purity and pollution are ways a community draws the lines of its world, marking what belongs where. The risk is that purity becomes a weapon, used to exclude certain people as unclean, and the great reformers have always fought that use. But the instinct beneath it is a good one. A holy act needs a real body, real water, real wood. We are not spirits floating above the world. We meet the sacred through matter, and matter, handled with care, becomes a kind of prayer.",
