        "advanced": "Materials and purity practices in sacred architecture and ritual: uncut altar stones (Exodus 20:25; Deuteronomy 27:5-6) and the hammerless building of the Temple (1 Kings 6:7); Islamic wudu and ghusl; Jewish mikveh and the laws of tumah and taharah; Shinto misogi and temizu; Hindu snana; and the use of stone, wood, gold, incense and beeswax across traditions. Mary Douglas's Purity and Danger (1966) interprets pollution rules as classification systems for social order. Experimental psychologists, including Chen-Bo Zhong and Katie Liljenquist (2006), reported a Macbeth effect linking physical cleansing with moral cleansing, though replication has been mixed. Jonathan Haidt connects purity and disgust to moral intuition. The limit is that purity regimes have excluded women, outcasts and minorities, and prophetic critiques, from Isaiah to Jesus's debates on purity to Dalit reformers, challenged this use. The topic ties sacred space to the body, nature and ritual."
