        "advanced": "Emile Durkheim introduced the conscience collective in The Division of Labour in Society (1893) for the shared beliefs and sentiments that bind a community, and in The Elementary Forms of Religious Life (1912) described collective effervescence, the heightened shared emotion of ritual that he took to be the source of the sacred. Carl Jung proposed the collective unconscious, inherited archetypal structures in the psyche, a view that is influential in culture and not accepted in empirical psychology. John Searle's account of social reality explains how money, borders and marriage exist because people collectively accept them, and Studies of synchrony in groups, such as singing and dancing together, show increased trust. Counterpoint: a collective has no mind of its own beyond individuals, so the term describes patterns of shared meaning and not a literal group consciousness. It ties to mythology, religion and love: a ritual works because many people feel the same thing at the same time."
