        "advanced": "The Flower of Life is a pattern of overlapping circles of equal size, each centered on the intersection of others, forming a hexagonal lattice. The best-known ancient example is at the Temple of Osiris in Abydos, Egypt, drawn in red ochre, though its date is uncertain, and examples occur in Assyria, India, China and Renaissance Europe. The modern esoteric claims that the pattern encodes the creation of the universe came largely from the New Age writer Drunvalo Melchizedek in the 1990s, and have no historical support. The mathematics is simple and real: the pattern arises from repeatedly drawing a circle at each intersection with a compass, and the hexagonal lattice is the most efficient way to tile circles on a plane, as in a honeycomb. The geometry is older than any one culture, but it is the meaning that people supply. Where this meets Art, it is a pattern that every culture with a compass has found."
