        "advanced": "There are exactly five convex polyhedra whose faces are identical regular polygons and whose corners are identical: tetrahedron, cube, octahedron, dodecahedron and icosahedron. Euclid proves this in Book 13 of the Elements, and the argument is short: at each vertex the face angles must add up to less than 360 degrees, which allows only five combinations. Plato, in the Timaeus, linked four of the solids to fire, earth, air and water, and the fifth to the cosmos. Kepler in 1596 tried to nest them to explain the planetary orbits, a model that failed but led him toward his true laws. In nature the icosahedron appears in the capsids of many viruses, as Caspar and Klug explained in 1962, because it builds a closed shell from few identical parts. Carbon-60 is a related form. Claims that crystals are Platonic solids are loose, since salt forms cubes but most minerals do not. Where this meets Philosophy, it is a case of a truth about possible forms that was proved by thought alone."
