        "advanced": "The Sri Yantra, or Sri Chakra, is the central diagram of the Sri Vidya tradition of Shaktism. Nine interlocking triangles, four pointing up, representing Shiva, and five pointing down, representing Shakti, create forty-three smaller triangles around a central dot called the bindu, surrounded by lotus petals and a square gate. Texts such as the Tantraraja Tantra and Saundarya Lahari, the latter attributed to Shankara, describe it, and it is used in worship and meditation as a map of the cosmos and of the body. Its geometry is precise, and drawing it with all lines meeting correctly in triples is a known difficulty. The link to cymatics, patterns formed by sound on plates, is popular, and Hans Jenny did produce striking cymatic figures, but there is no evidence that the Sri Yantra emerges from a particular sound such as om. The yantra works as a focus for attention: the eye travels inward through the layers toward the bindu. Where this meets Religion, it is a diagram used as a prayer."
