        "advanced": "The pineal gland is a small pinecone-shaped organ in the center of the brain that produces melatonin in response to darkness, regulating sleep cycles. In some reptiles and amphibians a parietal eye, a light-sensing structure on the top of the head, develops from nearby tissue, and in birds and fish the pineal itself is directly light-sensitive. In mammals light information arrives via the retina and the suprachiasmatic nucleus. Descartes proposed in 1649 that the pineal gland was the seat of the soul, and the image of the third eye, the ajna chakra, appears in Hindu and Buddhist art. The claim that the practice of meditation strengthens a third eye is a metaphor. What can be supported is that training in focused attention, visualization and body awareness reshapes cortical regions, in line with plasticity research. Where this meets Religion, it is the ancient place on the forehead where traditions ask the attention to rest."
