        "advanced": "The human eye responds to a narrow band of the electromagnetic spectrum, roughly 380 to 750 nanometers, and the ear to sound between about 20 and 20,000 hertz, so we sense only a sliver of what physically exists, while other animals sense more: bees see ultraviolet, some snakes sense infrared and elephants hear infrasound. Jakob von Uexküll called each animal's perceived world its Umwelt. Within the senses, the brain filters: thalamic gating, attention and prediction decide what reaches awareness, and Anil Seth describes perception as a controlled hallucination, a best guess from the brain constrained by input. Sensory deprivation produces hallucinations in some people within minutes to hours, as in Charles Bonnet syndrome among the visually impaired and in isolation tank studies, and overload can overwhelm the filter. Aldous Huxley's The Doors of Perception in 1954 popularized the reducing valve idea from Henri Bergson and C. D. Broad. The valve is a metaphor with a real physiology. Where this meets Philosophy, it recalls Kant's claim that we know only appearances."
