        "advanced": "Set and setting were named by Timothy Leary, Ralph Metzner and Richard Alpert in the 1960s: set is the mind a person brings, their expectations, mood and intentions, and setting is the physical and social environment. The idea has strong empirical support. Walter Pahnke's Good Friday experiment in 1962 gave psilocybin or a placebo to divinity students in a chapel, and most who received psilocybin reported mystical experiences, though the design had flaws. Studies by Roland Griffiths at Johns Hopkins report that preparation, guidance and a supportive room affect whether experience is meaningful and whether people are helped, and that mystical-type experiences predict benefit. Placebo research shows that expectation changes pain and mood in any context. Safety matters: in bad settings the same experience can be frightening. Ceremony, in every culture's use of powerful states, is the container that has been found to work. Where this meets Religion, it is what ritual is for."
