        "songs": [
          {
            "title": "Saturn",
            "artist": "Stevie Wonder"
          }
        ],
        "advanced": "Jung argued that visions and dreams in extreme states draw on archetypes: the shadow, the self, the mandala, the tree of life. In studies of psychedelic experience, recurring geometric forms appear: Heinrich Klüver in the 1920s catalogued form constants in mescaline visions, spirals, lattices, tunnels and cobwebs, and Jack Cowan and Paul Bressloff later explained them as patterns arising in the structure of visual cortex itself. That is a neural account, not a symbolic one, and it shows that universal imagery can arise from universal biology. David Lewis-Williams argued that the geometric and animal forms in Paleolithic cave art may reflect altered states, a controversial thesis. Jung's idea that visions are translations has support as a way of reading, and meaning is added by the person afterward. The images are also partly cultural, and a vision is not evidence about the outer world. Where this meets Art, it is the common vocabulary behind a cave wall and a cathedral window."
