        "advanced": "In Childhood and Society in 1950 Erik Erikson described eight stages of a life, and the seventh, in midlife, turns on a contest between generativity and stagnation. The Haudenosaunee Great Law of Peace is often cited for the principle of looking seven generations ahead when making decisions. The exact wording in popular quotations is uncertain, but the habit of counting unborn people among the stakeholders is real and old. Philosophy has come to the same place by argument: Hans Jonas in 1979 asked what we owe to those who cannot yet speak, and Derek Parfit showed how strange the logic of future persons becomes. A limit worth stating is that legacy-hunting can turn into vanity or pressure on the young, and many generative lives leave no children at all. Nature shows the shape in a tree planted by someone who will never sit in its shade. You are the middle of a long sentence, not its last word."
