        "advanced": "In 1954 Muzafer Sherif ran the Robbers Cave experiment, placing two groups of boys at a camp in Oklahoma. Within days they were hostile rivals. What finally dissolved it was not a lecture or a party but a superordinate goal, a problem neither group could solve alone, such as a broken water supply. Working together on it did what contact alone had failed to do. Émile Durkheim described the grown-up version in 1893 as organic solidarity, in which a society holds together not through sameness but through a division of labour pointed at a shared end. The limit is that purpose is a neutral amplifier. A mob, a cartel and a cult are bonded by shared aims too, so cohesion tells you how strong a group is, never whether it is good. The mythology door has the oldest crew in the story-book, the Argonauts, who each brought a different gift to one voyage. People stop feeling alone when they are needed for the same thing."
