        "advanced": "Margaret Stroebe and Henk Schut proposed the Dual Process Model in 1999. It describes two kinds of coping with bereavement. Loss orientation means facing the death directly, with crying, yearning and remembering. Restoration orientation means dealing with a changed life: new roles, tasks and identity. Their key claim is that healthy mourners oscillate between the two, and that time away from grief is part of the work, not a failure of it. Rigid commitment to one pole causes its own trouble, whether it is endless immersion in the loss or an anxious flight into busyness. The limit is that the model describes patterns rather than prescribes, and the research behind it is mostly correlational. The mythology door has an old picture of this movement in Persephone, who goes down to the dark and comes back to the light, and is the same person in both. You are allowed to laugh at lunch. It does not mean you stopped loving."
