        "advanced": "In 1997 Suzanne Simard published in Nature the finding that radioactive carbon labelled in one tree species reached a neighbouring tree of another species through underground fungal networks, and the press called it the wood wide web. The picture is more disputed than popular books suggest. In 2023 Justine Karst, Melanie Jones and Jason Hoeksema reviewed the evidence in Nature Ecology and Evolution and concluded that claims that these networks transfer resources to the benefit of seedlings, or that trees send warning signals through them, were stronger in the telling than in the data. Fungal links are real and well documented, but their ecological role remains an open question. The mysticism door has an older and quieter image in the Buddhist net of Indra, in which every jewel at every knot reflects all the others. You do not need fungus to show that nothing lives alone. A forest and a village both depend on what passes between."
