        "advanced": "The first law of thermodynamics, worked out by Julius Mayer, James Joule and Hermann von Helmholtz in the 1840s, says that energy changes form but is never created or destroyed. Applied to love it is a metaphor, and an honest one has to say where it fails: if you give away a dollar or an hour, you have a dollar or an hour less. What does not behave that way is the meaning of the gift. Marcel Mauss argued in The Gift in 1925 that giving creates a bond that circulates through a community and returns in forms nobody counted. In 2008 Elizabeth Dunn, Lara Aknin and Michael Norton found that spending money on others made people happier than spending it on themselves, and Aknin's 2013 study found the pattern in countries rich and poor. The mathematics door explains the structure as a non-zero-sum game, in which one side's gain does not need to be the other's loss. Giving moves value into a new form where both can hold it."
