        "advanced": "In 1984 Gerald Wilkinson reported in Nature that vampire bats in Costa Rica regurgitate blood to roost-mates that failed to feed, and that the donors were not only relatives but unrelated bats that had shared with them before. A bat can starve in about two days without a meal, so the loss for a well-fed donor is small and the gain for a starving recipient is great. Gerald Carter and Wilkinson showed in 2013 that bats given food by a roost-mate were more likely to return the help later, and that past help predicted giving better than kinship alone. Martin Nowak's 2006 paper listed five mechanisms by which cooperation evolves: kin selection, direct reciprocity, indirect reciprocity, network reciprocity and group selection. They work only when cheaters can be recognised and shut out, which is why the bats keep track. The mathematics door holds the same structure in game theory. Helping others is not opposed to self-interest. In the long run, it is what a clear-sighted self-interest looks like."
