        "advanced": "Marcus Raichle and colleagues identified the default mode network in 2001: a set of regions most active when a mind is at rest, wandering, remembering and thinking about itself. Judson Brewer found in 2011 that experienced meditators showed lower activity in these regions, and in 2012 Robin Carhart-Harris reported that psilocybin reduced activity and coordination within them, a pattern that tracked users' reports of a loosened sense of self. Roland Griffiths' 2006 studies at Johns Hopkins found that a single dose of psilocybin could bring about experiences of unity that volunteers rated among the most meaningful of their lives. The default mode network is not the ego and does not equal it, and the link between activity and experience is correlational. The mysticism door has the image in the Sufi and Vedantic tradition of the drop that becomes the ocean, where nothing is lost but a boundary. The self is a useful tool. It is not your whole size."
