        "advanced": "A farmer who plants seed must first decide not to eat it. Historians of agriculture such as Slicher van Bath found that in medieval Europe a sown grain of wheat often returned only a handful of grains at harvest, so a poor year could leave a family choosing between sowing and starving. Even so, the return on buried grain, in a decent season, outran every other form of saving, because it was living and multiplied itself. The Gospel of John states the paradox in 12:24: unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains alone, but if it dies, it bears much fruit. The limit is that not every seed sprouts, and none grows without soil, water and a season, so planting is a risk and not a guarantee. A gift given only to get something back is an investment, not a seed. What you hold back stays small. What you bury has a chance to become a field."
