        "advanced": "In 2018 Carolyn Parkinson, Adam Kleinbaum and Thalia Wheatley scanned the brains of students in one graduate programme while they watched the same set of video clips, and found that the closer two people were as friends, the more alike their neural responses were, moment by moment. Uri Hasson's group showed earlier that when a listener understands a speaker, their brain activity falls into a lagging echo of the speaker's, and that the closer the match, the better the understanding. The limit is a question of direction. Friends may be drawn together because they already see alike, or they may come to see alike because they have spent years together, and the data cannot yet separate the two. The consciousness door holds the harder form of the question, whether one mind can ever know what another's experience is like. A friend is the nearest you get to seeing through someone else's eyes."
