        "advanced": "In 1950 Leon Festinger, Stanley Schachter and Kurt Back studied Westgate, a housing complex for married MIT students, and found that people were most likely to be friends with those who lived next door and next most likely with those two doors down. In 1974 Mortimer Segal found that police cadets were most likely to befriend those whose surnames were alphabetically near their own, because they were seated in that order. Robert Zajonc's work on mere exposure in 1968 explains part of why: repeated contact with something harmless makes it more liked. The limit is that proximity cuts both ways. Ebbesen and colleagues showed in 1976 that the people we dislike also tend to live near us, and nearness only creates the chance for friendship, not the friendship. The nature door offers the plain image of a seed, which grows where it falls. Many of your closest friends began as whoever happened to be there."
