        "advanced": "In 2018 Anna Bruk, Sabine Scholl and Herbert Bless named the beautiful mess effect, after finding that people see another person's vulnerable disclosure as brave and warm, yet see their own as weak and embarrassing. We misjudge the cost of our own openness, which is part of why loneliness persists: each person stands behind their armour, assuming everyone else is more at ease. Irwin Altman and Dalmas Taylor's social penetration theory of 1973 described how relationships deepen like peeling an onion, with disclosure moving from public layers toward private ones, step by reciprocal step. The limit is that openness has to be matched to trust. Most of us are not too exposed but not exposed enough, and the right amount is to go first with something small. The mythology door has the wounded healer, the figure of Chiron, a centaur whose wound never healed and who taught healing, a pattern Carl Jung made central. The crack in you is what another person can hold on to."
