        "advanced": "In 1935 Einstein, Podolsky and Rosen argued that quantum mechanics must be incomplete, since it allowed two particles to share a state in which measuring one instantly fixes the other, however far apart. In 1964 John Bell showed that the question could be settled by experiment, and in 1982 Alain Aspect's measurements, and in 2015 loophole-free tests, including one at Delft, found that nature does behave as quantum theory says. First, entanglement cannot carry a message, because the outcome of each measurement looks random until the two records are compared, so there is no faster-than-light signal. Second, it is a feature of tiny, isolated systems, and bumping into the environment destroys it quickly, so there is no evidence of entanglement between two people, however they feel about each other. The mysticism door has an older picture of the same intuition in the Buddhist net of Indra, where every jewel contains every other. The physics says that parts of one system cannot be fully described apart."
