        "advanced": "The heart's electrical activity was first recorded from the body surface by Augustus Waller in 1887, and in 1963 its magnetic field was measured, founding magnetocardiography. The field is real, though far weaker than popular accounts suggest: it falls off quickly with distance, is about a million times weaker than Earth's own magnetic field at the chest, and needs extremely sensitive instruments to detect, even when close. The claim that one person's heart field can be read by another's brain, advanced by the HeartMath Institute, has not been established by independent work. What has is that people do influence one another through ordinary channels. In 2018 Pavel Goldstein and colleagues found that when a partner held a hand in pain, their brain activity synchronised, and the more empathic the partner, the more the pain eased. The science door has Faraday's discovery of induction, which taught that unseen fields are real and still demand evidence. You do not need a radio to explain why a hand on your shoulder helps."
