        "advanced": "In 1665 Christiaan Huygens, lying ill, noticed that two pendulum clocks hung on the same beam had fallen into step, swinging in opposite directions, and he correctly guessed that tiny vibrations through the wood were nudging each. Steven Strogatz's Sync traces the same phenomenon through fireflies flashing together, heart pacemaker cells and neurons, and the mathematician Yoshiki Kuramoto in 1975 wrote the model for how coupled oscillators lock together. Donn Byrne's work from 1971 showed that people like those who share their attitudes, but a 2008 meta-analysis by Matthew Montoya, Robert Horton and Jeffrey Kirchner found that perceived similarity drives attraction far more than actual similarity. The limit is that the so-called law of resonance in self-help writing is not physics, and it risks blaming people for who has not found them. The mathematics door holds the true law: coupled systems that interact can synchronise, with no master clock. Two clocks on one beam did not decide to agree. They shared a surface."
