        "advanced": "Pheromones are well proven in animals. In 1959 Adolf Butenandt isolated bombykol, the compound a female silk moth releases to call males, after extracting it from about half a million moths, and a male can respond to a few molecules. Claus Wedekind's 1995 study had women rate the smell of T-shirts worn by men, and found a preference for men whose immune genes, the MHC, differed from their own. Later studies have not held it up well: a 2017 meta-analysis by Winternitz and colleagues found no general support in humans for choosing MHC-dissimilar partners, though it found a preference for MHC-diverse ones. Tristram Wyatt, a leading pheromone biologist, argued in 2015 that no human pheromone has been clearly identified. Even so, smell matters in attraction and memory, since the olfactory system connects directly to the amygdala and hippocampus, and people can recognise their babies and partners by scent. The nature door has the silk moth, which has a language older than any word. Smell is the oldest sense, and its meanings are still being decoded."
