        "advanced": "In 1999 Tanya Chartrand and John Bargh described the chameleon effect: people unconsciously copy the posture, gestures and mannerisms of those they talk to, and being mimicked makes them like the mimic more and feel the conversation went smoothly. In 2011 Fabian Ramseyer and Wolfgang Tschacher filmed therapy sessions and found that the amount of movement synchrony between therapist and client predicted how clients rated the relationship and the outcome. Whether mirror neurons are the mechanism is unsettled, since Gregory Hickok has argued the evidence for their social role is thin, but the behaviour is solid either way. The limit is that mimicry works when it is natural and unnoticed, and it backfires when it is spotted or when the people mimicked feel excluded. The art door has the oldest name for this impulse in mimesis, which Aristotle in the Poetics called a natural human instinct and a source of pleasure. Attraction is partly the pleasure of being mirrored."
