        "advanced": "Phi, about 1.618, is the number that satisfies the proportion where the whole is to the larger part as the larger is to the smaller. In 1992 Stéphane Douady and Yves Couder showed in a physical model that when new seeds form one at a time where there is most room, the turn between them settles near 137.5 degrees, the golden angle, giving the spirals of sunflowers and pine cones. Mario Livio's The Golden Ratio in 2002 found that the Parthenon, the pyramids and the nautilus shell do not follow phi, and Pallett, Link and Lee found in 2010 that the most attractive faces were near average proportions, not golden ones. The limit is that we are drawn to symmetry, health and averageness, and phi is a minor case. The mathematics door has the reason: phi is the number hardest to approximate with fractions, so no seeds line up into gaps. Beauty is often the look of something that works."
