        "advanced": "Carl Jung described synchronicity in a 1952 essay, written in conversation with the physicist Wolfgang Pauli, as an acausal connecting principle, a meaningful link between an inner state and an outer event with no causal route between them. In 1989 Persi Diaconis and Frederick Mosteller published a paper on coincidences that set out the law of truly large numbers: with enough people and enough events, remarkable-seeming coincidences are bound to occur daily somewhere. The birthday problem shows it: in a room of only twenty-three people the chance that two share a birthday is already better than even. The limit of Jung's idea is that it offers no way to test it, and a meaning felt is not a connection proved. The mathematics door explains the odds and leaves the question of why a coincidence moves us. Whether or not the universe winks, the mind that notices has to decide what to do."
