        "advanced": "Joseph Campbell's The Hero with a Thousand Faces in 1949 argued that hero stories across cultures share one pattern of departure, trial and return, which he called the monomyth. Sigmund Freud described in 1920 a repetition compulsion, a tendency to re-enact painful situations, and William Swann's self-verification theory finds that people tend to seek partners who see them as they see themselves, even when that view is negative. The limit is that the idea of karma, as a moral ledger running across lives, has no empirical support, and it has sometimes been used cruelly to tell the suffering that they earned it. The religion door holds the original doctrine. In Hindu and Buddhist thought, karma means action and its consequences, and the traditions largely stress that what you do shapes what you become. A pattern that returns is a pattern not yet understood. Once it is understood, it can finally be left."
